<abstract>
<para>&pdfsmithy; is a &PDF; editor for the &Linux; desktop. It reads
documents, corrects the words on a page, draws and fills in forms, arranges and
merges pages, reads scans, signs, stamps, comments, redacts, compresses, prints
and hands files to an archive. All of it happens on the machine it is installed
on. Nothing is uploaded anywhere.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>pdf-smithy</keyword>
<keyword>PDF</keyword>
<keyword>OCR</keyword>
<keyword>scan</keyword>
<keyword>redaction</keyword>
<keyword>signature</keyword>
<keyword>watermark</keyword>
<keyword>forms</keyword>
<keyword>PDF/A</keyword>
<keyword>preflight</keyword>
<keyword>privacy</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>&pdfsmithy; edits &PDF; documents. It opens one at reading size, where
you turn its pages, select and copy its text and fill in its form. From there
you can correct the words on the page, move what it draws, mark it up, draw a
form onto it, or step back and put its pages in a different order. It also
signs, stamps, blacks out for good, makes documents smaller, reads scans,
prints them as booklets and converts them for an archive.</para>

<para>It exists because of how that work is usually done. The quickest way to
merge two &PDF; files or turn a scan into searchable text is to find a website
that will do it, and the file in front of you is an employment contract, a
payslip, a tenancy agreement or a letter from a hospital. Uploading any of
those to a stranger's server in order to rotate a page is not a reasonable
thing to be asked to do, and it is what most people end up doing, because the
alternative was five separate tools that do not know about each other. Nothing
in &pdfsmithy; leaves this machine. There is no account and no upload. The only
time it touches the network at all is when you ask it to open or save a
document on a share you named yourself, and even then the file is fetched,
edited here and copied back.</para>

<sect1 id="introduction-modes">
<title>Four modes, and why the program is built on them</title>

<para>What you are here to do is the first thing the program asks, and it is
the only steering it has. Four buttons sit at the left of the toolbar, one of
them always pressed, and the one that is pressed decides which view is in the
middle of the window, what a click on the page means, which second toolbar is
up, and what the status bar says at the bottom.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F5</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Read &amp; Fill In</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Turn the pages, select and copy text, and fill in forms.
Nothing can be changed by accident. This is where the program starts, because a
document is something you open to read; changing it is a decision, and a
decision should take a click. See <xref linkend="reading"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F6</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit the Page</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Correct the words, move the pictures, mark the page up. The
same page, at the same size, with handles on it. See
<xref linkend="page-editing"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F7</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit the Form</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Draw form fields onto the page, move them and set what they do.
Only the fields answer the mouse here, so a field cannot be dropped on a
paragraph and take it with it. See <xref linkend="form-editing"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F8</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Arrange the Pages</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Reorder, turn, duplicate and throw away whole pages. The
document appears as a grid of thumbnails instead of as a page.
See <xref linkend="arranging"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The reason for making this explicit, rather than letting it follow from
whichever tool was last pressed, is that <quote>can I break this document by
clicking on it</quote> is a question you ought to be able to answer by looking
at the window. A program with six independent switches cannot answer it.</para>

<para>Three of the four modes show the same document at the same size, so
changing between them moves nothing: the page you were on, the size you had set
and the line you had reached are all where you left them. Opening another
document does not change the mode either. Somebody who has said they are here
to arrange pages is still here to arrange pages when the second file
arrives.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="introduction-window">
<title>What the window shows</title>

<para>There are two rows of toolbars and never more. The top row is the same in
every mode: opening, saving, printing, undoing, the four mode buttons, and two
buttons with arrows on them, <guibutton>Zoom</guibutton> and
<guibutton>Sidebars</guibutton>, holding the zoom levels and the side panels.
The row under it belongs to the mode and changes with it, so the tools on
screen are the tools that do something to the document on screen.</para>

<para>The status bar says which mode is in force, in words, and holds a box
saying which page of how many is showing. Type a number into that box to go to
that page. Beside it is how many pages the document has and how many of them
are selected. In <guimenuitem>Arrange the Pages</guimenuitem> a slider appears
at the right-hand end for the size of the thumbnails; it is hidden in the other
three modes, where there is no grid for it to act on.</para>

<para>Three side panels can be opened and closed independently:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F9</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Page Thumbnails</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>A narrow strip of pages beside the document, marking the one
you are on and travelling with you as you scroll. It hides itself while the
page grid is up, because the grid already shows every page and a second strip
of the same thing costs a third of the window twice.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>B</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Contents Sidebar</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>The document's table of contents, which can be read, followed
and rewritten. See <xref linkend="contents"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F4</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Properties Sidebar</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Names whatever you last clicked and lists its attributes.
See <xref linkend="properties"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>A fourth panel appears when a tool that works on the page is opened
(trimming, redacting, signing, watermarking, stamping numbers, or filling in a
form as a list). It puts the page where the grid was and its own controls at
the edge of the window. Closing that panel is the same as cancelling, and you
are asked whether to keep what you have done first.</para>

<para>All four are ordinary &kde; docks: drag the title bar to another edge,
tear one off to float it on a second screen, or drop one on top of another so
that they share their space as tabs. The window remembers where you left them,
so every tool from then on opens where you put the last one.</para>

<para>The menu bar can be hidden with
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show Menubar</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
The button at the right-hand end of the top toolbar then holds every menu, so
nothing becomes unreachable.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="introduction-collecting">
<title>Work is collected, then kept</title>

<para>Typing on a page, dragging a picture about, marking a paragraph up,
drawing a form field and answering a form all collect rather than write. Until
they are written down, the page underneath still shows the document as it was
opened.</para>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Enter;</keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Keep the Changes on the Page</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
writes them into the document as one undo step, and saving does it for you.
Losing an afternoon of typing to a
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo> would be the worst
bug this program could have.</para>

<para>The reason for collecting is that a page's worth of corrections should be
one undo step rather than one per keystroke, and that a page can only be
rewritten in a single pass. Anything that had to be refused, or could not be
made to fit, is said in words at the moment the changes are kept.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="introduction-lossless">
<title>Lossless by default</title>

<para>Reordering a scanned contract does not re-encode it. &pdfsmithy; works on
the document's object structure rather than on pictures of its pages, so the
pages that come out of a save are byte for byte the pages that went in, and
saving the same document twice produces identical files.</para>

<para>Editing a page works the same way. Correcting a line or moving a picture
rewrites the page's instruction stream rather than rebuilding the page, so
everything that was not touched comes out exactly as it went in: the same
fonts, the same image bytes, the same vector art at the same precision. That is
what makes it safe to fix a typo in a scanned contract and still be able to say
that nothing else about the document changed.</para>

<para>Where an operation cannot avoid changing the page, compressing its images
or redacting a picture for instance, it says so before it does it, and this
handbook says so in the chapter concerned.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="introduction-several-documents">
<title>Several documents at once</title>

<para>&pdfsmithy; holds any number of opened files and one ordered list of
pages drawn from them. Merging is therefore the ordinary state of affairs
rather than a special command: insert a second file and its pages join the
grid, where they can be dragged in among the pages that were already there.
Reordering a two-thousand-page document costs nothing, because no page is
copied to do it.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="reading">
<title>Reading a Document</title>

<para>This is the mode the program starts in and the one a document opens into.
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F5</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Read &amp; Fill In</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
comes back to it from anywhere. Text can be dragged over, selected and copied,
form fields can be filled in, and the comments the document carries are drawn
and can be read. Nothing about the document can be altered by accident, which
is what makes it safe to hand somebody a program that can alter
everything.</para>

<sect1 id="reading-opening">
<title>Opening a document</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
opens a document, replacing whatever was in the window. The file dialog is
&kde;'s own, so a document on a network share can be opened as easily as one on
the disk; it is fetched, edited here, and copied back where it came from when
you save. <guimenuitem>Open Recent</guimenuitem> holds the last files you
worked on.</para>

<para>Dragging &PDF; files from the file manager onto the window opens the
first of them into an empty window and appends them to a document that is
already open. Dropping them between two pages of the page grid puts them
exactly there, which is the quickest way to merge two documents.</para>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>N</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>New</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
starts a document with one blank page, so the program can be started at rather
than only opened into. It has no file until you save it, which is why
<guimenuitem>Save</guimenuitem> asks where it should go.</para>

<sect2 id="reading-password">
<title>A document that asks for a password</title>

<para>A locked file is a question rather than a failure. &pdfsmithy; asks for
the password that opens it, saying which file it is about, and asks again as
often as it takes; a wrong answer says so plainly instead of showing the same
prompt a second time with nothing to explain it. Somebody typing a long
password off a card gets it wrong occasionally, and one refusal with no way
back would shut them out of a document this very program can lock.</para>

<para>The password is kept for as long as the document is open, and saving
locks the file again with it. Without that, a plain
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo> would quietly
write an unprotected copy over a document its owner had protected. Opening a
different document forgets it, so a password is never carried into a file that
never had one. To change or remove it, see <xref linkend="document-password"/>.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-moving">
<title>Moving about</title>

<para>The pages run down the window one under another at reading size. The page
that owns the middle of the window is the one you are said to be on, rather
than the topmost one, which on a tall page is the one you finished with two
screens ago.</para>

<para>Scrolling glides rather than jumps: the wheel, <keycap>Page Down</keycap>
and <keycap>Page Up</keycap> all travel a little less than a windowful, so a
line or two of what you have already read stays on screen and the eye has somewhere to
land. A touchpad is not glided, because the distance the fingers travelled is
already as smooth as the hand that made it, and gliding it as well would put
the page behind the fingers.</para>

<para>The box in the status bar says which page is showing. Type a number into
it to go there. The <guimenu>View</guimenu> menu and the reading toolbar hold
<guimenuitem>First Page</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Home;</keycombo>),
<guimenuitem>Previous Page</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Next Page</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Last Page</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&End;</keycombo>).</para>

<note><para><keycap>Page Up</keycap> and <keycap>Page Down</keycap>
deliberately have no menu command attached to them. A window-wide shortcut is
matched before the key reaches the page, so claiming those two keys would have
made the gliding motion the document view is built around unreachable.</para></note>

<para>The thumbnail strip
(<menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F9</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Page Thumbnails</guimenuitem></menuchoice>)
answers <quote>where am I in this file</quote> in a way a page number cannot. A
number tells you nothing about a two-hundred-page report; a strip of pictures
tells you that the chapter you want is the one with the table in it, four
thumbnails down. Click a thumbnail to go to its page. The strip marks the page
you are on and travels smoothly with the scrolling, so reading slowly down one
tall page does not leave it standing still long enough to look broken. Its own
slider and its two buttons make the thumbnails wider or narrower.</para>

<para>The contents sidebar
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>B</keycap></keycombo>) lists the
document's bookmarks and follows you through it. See
<xref linkend="contents"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-zoom">
<title>Sizing the pages to the window</title>

<para>The number you usually want is not a number. The
<guimenu>View</guimenu> menu, and the <guibutton>Zoom</guibutton> button on the
top toolbar, offer five answers instead:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Fit Width</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>1</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>The page as wide as the window.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Fit Height</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>2</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>A whole page down the window, allowed to be wider than the
window if it must.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>One Whole Page</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>One page, both ways.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Two Pages Side by Side</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>4</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>Pages laid out as spreads, sized so that two of them really do
fit.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Free Zoom</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>0</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>The size stays where it is and the window stops claiming
it.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>These are modes rather than one-off jumps: resize the window, open a
panel or turn a page to landscape, and the fit is worked out again. Turning the
wheel with &Ctrl; held, or using
<guimenuitem>Larger Pages</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Smaller Pages</guimenuitem>, means you have overruled the fit, and
the size stays where you put it. One of the five is always ticked, and it is
the one describing the view you are actually looking at.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Two Pages Side by Side in
the Document</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is the spread layout without a word
about the size, for a reader who has already set a size they are happy with. It
stays ticked while <guimenuitem>Two Pages Side by Side</guimenuitem> is in
force, since that is the same layout with the size worked out to match.</para>

<para>The page grid and the document each keep a size of their own, and the
five commands act on whichever of the two is on screen. Arranging pages and
reading them are different jobs at different sizes, and a single number shared
between them would mean every trip to the grid resized the document and
back.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-dragging">
<title>What a drag does</title>

<para>Three answers to one question, in the <guimenu>View</guimenu> menu and
behind the <guibutton>Drag</guibutton> button on the reading toolbar. That
button wears whichever of the three is in hand, so what a drag will do can be
answered by looking.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Select Text</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Drag across words to select and copy them.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Select an Area</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Drag a box and take the text and the picture inside
it.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Move the Page</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Drag the page about.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Holding <keycap>Space</keycap> moves the page without leaving the tool
you have in hand.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-text">
<title>Selecting and copying text</title>

<para>With <guimenuitem>Select Text</guimenuitem> in hand, drag across the page
to mark text. The pointer becomes an I-beam only where there really is text
under it, so a scan says plainly that there is nothing to select, until text
recognition has been run over it, after which the invisible layer it added
marks and copies like any other text (<xref linkend="scans"/>). A double click
picks the word under it, a selection carries on across pages so that a
quotation running over a page break comes out in one piece,
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>C</keycap></keycombo> copies what is
marked, and &Esc; clears it.</para>

<para>The marking moves a word at a time rather than a letter at a time,
because a word is the unit the renderer reports and half of one cannot be asked
for. The spaces and the line breaks in what you copy are the renderer's own
rather than guessed from where the letters sit, which is why a word broken
across a hyphen does not acquire a space in the middle and two words set tight
against one another do not run together.</para>

<para>Dragging over a line that happens to be highlighted still selects the
words underneath it. Copying a quoted sentence out of a marked-up contract is
the reason the marks were made in the first place.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-area">
<title>Taking a picture off the page</title>

<para><guimenuitem>Select an Area</guimenuitem> draws a box instead. What is
inside it, the text and the picture both, goes to the clipboard, so a diagram
can be pasted into a mail or a report without exporting anything.
<xref linkend="export-images"/> is the command for whole pages.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-forms">
<title>Filling in a form</title>

<para>A form is filled in where it stands on the page. Filling one in through a
list of labels somewhere else means reading the form twice, once on the page to
find out what is being asked and once in the list to find the box that asks it,
and three fields called <quote>Date</quote> are indistinguishable in a list and
obvious on the page.</para>

<para>What is underneath is the real document, appearance streams and all, so a
field that draws its own frame already looks like a field and nothing is added
to it. Drawn over it is only what the document does not say visibly:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>a pale wash, so that which parts of the sheet are to be filled
in can be seen at a glance;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a hairline round each field, so that a box which draws nothing
at all can be found;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a red outline round the fields the form marks as
required;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a grey dashed outline round the fields that will not take an
answer: the ones the form locks, and signature fields, which cannot be signed
from here. The pointer turns to the forbidden sign over them, which answers
<quote>why can I not type here</quote> before the click rather than after
it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Pointing at one button of a radio group deepens the wash on all of them,
which is how the group is shown to be a group. It answers the question that
matters before the click rather than after it: which other buttons will go off
when I press this one. Drawing a line from button to button instead would be
ink this program invented, laid across whatever the form printed between them,
which on a real form is the words of the choices themselves.</para>

<para>Click a text field and a real text box appears over it, at its size and
in its place. A tick box or a radio button gets no box at all: one click is the
whole interaction.</para>

<para>&Tab; walks the fields and <keycombo action="simul">&Shift;&Tab;</keycombo>
walks them backwards, in the order the form's author declared them and grouped
by page rather than in the order they happen to sit on the paper. Sorting by
position looks tidier and then walks a two-column form across the gutter, which
is exactly the thing people complain about. Fields that cannot take an answer
are left out of the walk, since a dead stop in a tab run is worse than a gap.
The walk wraps round at the end and the page scrolls to whichever field it
reaches. &Enter; finishes a one-line answer and moves on,
<keycap>Space</keycap> ticks the box that is picked, and &Esc; leaves the
field.</para>

<para>Nothing is written into the file as you type. The answers are held until
the changes on the page are kept or the document is saved, which is why a new
answer is drawn over a white patch rather than beside the old one. Filling a
field sets its value and asks for its appearance to be regenerated; that second
half matters, because a value with no appearance shows up blank in about half
the readers in use, including some of the ones that print.</para>

<sect2 id="reading-form-list">
<title>The same form as a list</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Forms</guimenu><guimenuitem>Fill In the Form as a
List…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is worth having when the form is long. It puts
the page on one side and a list of every field on the other, each with whatever
label its author wrote beside it and the page it sits on, so that a form can be
worked through from top to bottom without hunting for the next box. Clicking a
field in the list moves the page to it, and clicking a box on the page picks it
in the list. Required fields are marked, and the line under the list says how
many of the fields can be filled in and how many are locked. A document with no
form fields says so rather than opening an empty list.</para>

<para>A field the form locks is shown and refused rather than left out: on the
page it is outlined in grey dashes, and in the list it is there but greyed and
says that the form locks it. Hiding it would leave you wondering whether you
had missed a question.</para>

<para>The panel also offers to make the answers permanent, which draws the
fields as they stand and removes the interactivity. That is what to do before
sending a filled form back, for the same reason as above: what a form looks
like when its values have no appearance is up to the reader, and flattening
ends the argument. It cannot be undone in the finished file, so keep the
fillable version if you might need to change an answer. Answers typed on the
page are written as answers and nothing more; the form stays a form. Making it
permanent is a deliberate act and has its own switch.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reading-comments">
<title>Reading somebody else's comments</title>

<para>Every comment the document carries is drawn here, can be clicked to bring
its attributes into the properties sidebar, and a note still gives up its text
when it is clicked. Nothing can be drawn, moved or deleted, so a document can
be handed to somebody in this mode without its markup being at risk. Making
marks of your own is <xref linkend="page-comments"/>.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="page-editing">
<title>Editing the Page</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F6</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit the Page</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
makes the page editable where it stands: click into a line and type, drag a
picture, pull a handle, mark a paragraph up. It is the same page at the same
size as the one you were reading.</para>

<para>Three layers are live at once and they are asked in a fixed order. A pen
in hand wins the press, because that is what you have told the program you
are doing. Then the words, because a click on a line of text means the words
rather than the block. The block comes last, and the handles round it are what
moving is for.</para>

<para>Nothing here is written as you work. See
<xref linkend="page-keeping"/>.</para>

<sect1 id="page-correcting">
<title>Correcting the words</title>

<para>Every run of text that can be written into is outlined faintly before
anything is clicked, so the shape of what can be changed is visible rather than
discovered by surprise. Click into one and type. The caret opens where you
clicked rather than at the end of the line, which is the difference between
typing on a page and filling in a box that happens to sit on one.</para>

<para>A run is not a paragraph and often not even a whole line. A &PDF; page is
a list of instructions, one of which draws a run of glyphs at a coordinate, and
that run is the unit that can be replaced. What is outlined is therefore what
the file holds rather than what the layout looks like.</para>

<para>&Tab; moves the caret to the next run on the page that can be written
into and <keycombo action="simul">&Shift;&Tab;</keycombo> to the one before,
wrapping round at the bottom rather than stopping dead; tabbing is how somebody
checks a whole page, and a caret that dies at the bottom sends them back to the
mouse. &Enter; keeps what you typed and leaves the line, &Esc; puts the line
back as it was, and clicking somewhere else keeps it, which is what clicking
out of a cell does in every table anyone has used. While the caret is in a
line, <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>C</keycap></keycombo>,
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>X</keycap></keycombo> and
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>V</keycap></keycombo> mean the letters
in that line, as they do in any text box.</para>

<para>A third of a second after you stop typing, the edit is written to a
scratch copy of that one page and the page is drawn again from it. From then on
what is on the screen is the file rather than a picture of what the file will
say. It waits for the typing to stop because it costs about a tenth of a second
on a dense page.</para>

<sect2 id="page-correcting-box">
<title>The box grows, the letters do not shrink</title>

<para>Adding a word to a line makes the line longer. Narrowing the type to keep
the old width instead is what a layout program does when it has nowhere to put
the extra letters, and doing that by default would make every correction
quietly disfigure the page. So a run's box follows what is typed into it at the
size the page sets.</para>

<para>The exception is offered per line, on the padlock button beside the
caret. Pressed, it reads <quote>This line keeps its box: the type is narrowed
until the text fits inside it</quote>, which is what a table cell or a boxed
figure needs. Released, it reads <quote>This line's box grows with the
text</quote>. The properties sidebar says which of the two a line is set to
under <guilabel>Box</guilabel>.</para>

<para>Growing costs something, and it costs it immediately, because a &PDF;
page has no paragraphs and nothing beside a run moves aside to make room for
it. A line that has grown over its neighbour, or past where the rest of the
page's text stops, says so while you are typing: the paper under the caret
turns a warm colour, the outline turns orange, and the message names what has
happened. The three it can say are that the line now reaches past the edge of
the page where nothing is printed, that it reaches into the margin past where
the rest of the page's text stops, or what it has grown over. Finding that out
in the saved file instead would be finding it out too late.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-correcting-refusals">
<title>Two refusals, both while you are working</title>

<para>These arrive at the gesture rather than at saving time, and that
difference is the whole difference between a feature and a trap.</para>

<para>A run set in a font that cannot be written into is outlined with a dotted
line instead of a solid one, the pointer turns to the forbidden sign over it,
and clicking it says why on the spot. The reasons the page can give are that it
does not say which font the text uses, that the font does not say which
characters it holds (so replacing its text would produce the wrong letters), or
that some of the text uses characters the font does not explain.</para>

<para>A font that does not hold a character you have typed refuses that
character, naming it beside the line as you type. A font embedded in a &PDF;
usually carries only the characters that document actually used, so a document
which never contained a <quote>ü</quote> has no <quote>ü</quote> to draw, and
the line is left as it is rather than being given a blank or a wrong glyph you
would not notice. The Fonts tool can add the missing characters to the document
(<xref linkend="tools-fonts"/>), which is what lifts this limit.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-correcting-format">
<title>Setting a line differently</title>

<para>With the caret in a line, the properties sidebar offers its typeface, its
size, bold, italic and its colour, and a <guibutton>Set as the page
does</guibutton> button that puts all of it back. Each control writes through
the moment it is touched.</para>

<para>Formatting applies to the <emphasis>whole run</emphasis>. A &PDF;'s unit
of type is the run: one text-showing operation carries one font at one size,
and a page cannot express half a run in something else without being cut into
two operations. Selecting part of a line and restyling only that is therefore
refused rather than quietly applied to the lot.</para>

<para><guilabel>Bold</guilabel> and <guilabel>Italic</guilabel> are available
only once a different typeface has been chosen from the list, and say so in
their tooltip: the document carries one cut of its own face, so a bold or
italic of it has to come from a typeface chosen above. A cut is a separate font
programme, and there is no bold of the page's own face to reach for. A disabled
box that explains itself is better than a checkbox that does nothing.</para>

<para>The typeface is a list rather than a font picker for a related reason.
The page names a face such as <quote>Graphik-Semibold</quote>, which is a cut
and not a family and is not what this machine calls anything it has. Putting
that name into a font chooser would make it snap to some other family and read
as though you had asked for that. So what the page says is shown as a fact, and
asking for something else is a separate, deliberate choice; a different face
has to be found, cut down and carried inside the document, which is not the
same act as reading one. A face whose glyphs cannot be embedded from this
system is refused by name, because the alternative is a page of empty boxes
discovered far too late.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-correcting-limits">
<title>What correcting text cannot do</title>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>It does not reflow. There is no paragraph model in a &PDF;: a
page is a list of glyphs at coordinates, and nothing in the file records which
line follows which. Making a sentence longer therefore cannot push the next
line down, because there is no <quote>next line</quote> written down anywhere
to push.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A replacement that is too wide for a line whose box is being
kept is squeezed to fit, down to about three fifths of normal letter width and
no further, so that it does not run into whatever sits to its right. Letters at
a tenth of their width are not text any more. Anything still too long is named
afterwards rather than left for you to find.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A font embedded in a document usually carries only the
characters that document used, and asking for one it does not hold is refused
rather than drawn wrongly.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Text drawn inside an embedded drawing, and text that is part of
a scanned picture, cannot be changed this way. For a scan, what looks like text
is pixels, and the invisible layer that text recognition adds describes the
picture rather than being drawn from it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>What <emphasis>is</emphasis> promised is worth stating too: the
replacement is written in the page's own font, in the page's own instruction
stream, and everything else on the page is left byte for byte alone.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="page-objects">
<title>Moving and restyling what the page draws</title>

<para>A &PDF; page is a list of drawing instructions rather than a list of
objects. &pdfsmithy; invents the handle: every painting operation gets a
boundary that lights up under the pointer, and whatever is chosen gets eight
handles round it and a ninth above it to turn it by. A picture is moved by
dragging the picture, which is the one thing every other program on the machine
has taught you to try first.</para>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Move What the Page Draws…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
is the way in from the menu. It takes you to this mode with the properties
sidebar open. (It is deliberately not called <quote>Edit the Page…</quote>: one
menu bar carrying that name twice, for two different commands, is a menu bar
that cannot be read.)</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Choosing</term>
<listitem><para>Click something to take it. &Ctrl;-click adds to the choice.
Clicking something already chosen keeps the whole choice, so a group of five
can be dragged without picking them again one by one. A drag that starts on
bare paper bands a selection round several. &Esc; lets go.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Moving</term>
<listitem><para>Drag it. The arrow keys move what is chosen a point at a time,
and ten points with &Shift; held.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Scaling</term>
<listitem><para>Drag a corner to scale it keeping its proportions, or hold
&Shift; to free them. Drag the middle of an edge to change one axis only. The
far side stays put, which is what makes a handle feel attached to the object
rather than to the pointer.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Turning</term>
<listitem><para>Drag the round handle on the stalk above the box. Holding
&Shift; snaps to fifteen degrees.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Removing</term>
<listitem><para>&Del; or &Backspace; takes it off the page. While something on
the page is chosen those keys mean that thing, not the page.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Clicking a line of text offers to correct the words rather than to move
the block; moving is what the handles round it are for.</para>

<sect2 id="page-objects-clipboard">
<title>Cut, copy and paste</title>

<para>Copying an object does not duplicate an instruction; there is no way to
draw an existing operator a second time somewhere else. So a copy is
<emphasis>described</emphasis> well enough to be written from nothing: the
words of a line, the pixels of a picture. Pasting writes that description onto
the page as a new object, which is then an object like any other, with a
boundary, handles and gestures of its own. Each paste steps twelve points
further than the last, so pasting three times leaves three copies to be found
rather than three in one place looking like one.</para>

<para>What has no such description, a vector drawing or an embedded form, is
named at the cut rather than pasted wrong. Copied text is set again in a
standard font, so it will not match the letters that were already on the page
exactly, and that is said as well.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-objects-align">
<title>Lining several things up</title>

<para>From two objects upwards the properties sidebar grows a row of alignment
buttons, because the eye cannot put two left edges on the same line by dragging
and the arithmetic can.</para>

<para><guibutton>Align Left</guibutton>, <guibutton>Align Centre</guibutton>
and <guibutton>Align Right</guibutton> line the edges or the centres up
sideways; <guibutton>Align Top</guibutton>,
<guibutton>Align Middle</guibutton> and <guibutton>Align Bottom</guibutton> do
the same up and down. The line is taken from the box enclosing everything
chosen rather than from the first or the last of them, because a selection made
by dragging a band has no first. Nothing is resized.</para>

<para><guibutton>Space Out Sideways</guibutton> and <guibutton>Space Out
Upright</guibutton> put equal gaps between them; the outermost two stay where
they are, so distributing does not quietly change how much of the page the
group covers. Gaps are measured between edges rather than between centres,
which is what people mean and what looks right when the things are different
sizes. Three things are needed, since two have nothing between them.</para>

<para><guibutton>Same Width</guibutton>, <guibutton>Same Height</guibutton> and
<guibutton>Same Size</guibutton> give everything the size of the largest,
keeping each top left corner. Growing rather than shrinking, because a field
made too small loses its text while one made too big only looks
generous.</para>

<para>Anything the engine cannot move is left where it is and counted out loud
before anything moves, so <quote>four of the five lined up</quote> is not
something you have to work out by staring at the page.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-objects-limits">
<title>What moving things cannot do, and why</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>One waiting change per object</term>
<listitem><para>Giving something a colour takes the place of a move that was
waiting on the same object, and the window says so when it happens rather than
leaving you to find it in the file. The page is rewritten in a single pass and
every object number counts painting operations from the start of the page, so a
second pass would be addressed to a page the first one had already renumbered.
<menuchoice><guimenuitem>Undo the Change to This</guimenuitem></menuchoice> in
the right-click menu drops what is waiting on the chosen objects; ordinary undo
cannot reach it, because it is not in the document yet.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A flattened placement cannot be moved</term>
<listitem><para>An object whose placement is baked into the page cannot be
moved at all, since the arithmetic cannot be reversed. The refusal arrives at
the press.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>What lies on top of what cannot be changed yet</term>
<listitem><para>It would mean moving whole blocks of drawing operators about,
which is only safe when each block is properly enclosed, and on a real page
they often are not. The properties sidebar says where an object comes in the
painting order and says plainly that it cannot be changed, rather than offering
a control that does nothing.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A moved object travels on top of the page</term>
<listitem><para>What the layer draws over is a picture of the file as it
stands. The moment something is chosen, that picture is made twice: the page
with the chosen objects left out, and the chosen objects on their own. The
gesture then costs nothing at all, because it is only those two pictures being
drawn in new places. The consequence is visible and worth knowing: the object
travels on top of everything, so something that lay underneath it comes out
from under it as soon as it moves, and a highlight or a comment lying over it
stays where it is. That last one is right: the comment was put on the page, not
on the object. Scaling and turning are a bitmap transform until the gesture
settles; what makes the pixels exact for good is saving the document. Only one
page is held this way, so changes waiting on other pages are seen after the
save.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>An embedded drawing is one unit</term>
<listitem><para>Objects inside it cannot be reached without dissolving the
drawing first. Text is one object per drawing operation, which is usually a
line rather than a paragraph. A shape's clickable outline is approximated,
though what appears on the page is still drawn exactly as the file says, and
clipping is followed only as far as a rectangle.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="page-comments">
<title>Comments and the drawing tools</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guisubmenu>Mark Up</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Comments…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
takes you to the page with the highlighter in hand, the author set to the name
this machine knows you by, and the properties sidebar open. Marking up is done
to a document you are looking at, so there is nothing left for a separate
window to add.</para>

<para>The pens are on the page toolbar and in
<menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guisubmenu>Mark Up</guisubmenu></menuchoice>:
<guimenuitem>Pick</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Highlight</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Underline</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Strikethrough</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Rectangle</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Ellipse</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Line</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Freehand</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Text Box</guimenuitem> and <guimenuitem>Note</guimenuitem>. The
four shapes share the <guibutton>Shapes</guibutton> button on the toolbar,
which wears whichever of them was chosen last. A toolbar rather than a menu, because
choosing a pen is something done dozens of times in a session and never worth
going looking for.</para>

<sect2 id="page-comments-making">
<title>Making a mark</title>

<para>Drag along a line to mark it. What is marked is the band you dragged and
not the words underneath it, so drag along the line rather than expecting the
mark to find the text by itself. A drag that a steady hand kept too thin to see
is grown about the line it followed rather than refused. A drag of under three
points in both directions is treated as a slip and taken off the page
again.</para>

<para>A rectangle, an ellipse or a text box is dragged out as a box. A line
uses the two ends the drag actually had, so a line dragged up to the right is
not the same as one dragged down to the right. Freehand follows the pointer,
keeping a point about every point of travel rather than every mouse move, since
every one of those ends up in the file. A note is a single click: it is a fixed
small marker, and dragging one out would only make it an odd size.</para>

<para>What is being dragged out is drawn faintly and dashed, because it is a
proposal and should not be mistaken for a mark that is already on the page.
With any pen but <guimenuitem>Pick</guimenuitem> the pointer is a cross
everywhere on the page, which is what stops somebody trying to select a word
with a highlighter.</para>

<para>With <guimenuitem>Pick</guimenuitem>, click a mark to take it and drag it
to move it. &Del; removes it and &Esc; lets go. There are no resize handles: a
comment is moved, not scaled. A comment is allowed to hang over the edge of the
page it is about, because stopping it dead at the margin would feel like a
fault.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-comments-properties">
<title>What a comment says</title>

<para>The rest of a comment is in the properties sidebar, and unlike a form
field all of it can be changed, because a comment is yours. It holds what the
comment says, its colour, how thick its line is (marked-up text and notes take
no notice of that), what kind of mark it is, who made it, and which page it is
on. Setting the page carries the comment to that page and scrolls there.</para>

<para>Changing the kind keeps whatever the old one used, so changing your mind
twice comes back to where it started. Freehand is the one kind that cannot be
made out of a mark you dragged: there is no ink in a rectangle, and an empty
freehand comment draws nothing at all. A squiggly underline appears in the list
although no pen draws one, because a document may well carry one and a list
that cannot name what something is would be worse than one that cannot make
it.</para>

<para>A note gives up its text when it is clicked. Nothing else does, because
everything else already shows what it is about: a text box draws its own words,
and a highlight is the words it lies over.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-comments-beside">
<title>Beside the page, never in it</title>

<para>Comments are the one kind of markup that &PDF; has a proper place for.
They live beside the page rather than in its content stream, so adding, editing
and removing one leaves everything underneath completely untouched. That also
means a comment can be taken off again later without a trace, which is exactly
what a remark on somebody else's contract ought to be.</para>

<para>Every comment is written with its own appearance. The alternative, which
is to leave each reader to draw it from the properties, is permitted by the
specification and looks different in every program, and for a highlight over a
piece of evidence that is not a cosmetic matter.</para>

<para>Comments follow their page as the document is rearranged: moving a page,
inserting a file or turning a sheet carries its comments with it. The one case
that cannot be followed is a page deleted outright, and that is reported rather
than being allowed to happen quietly.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="page-comments-exchange">
<title>Sending remarks without sending the document</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guisubmenu>Mark Up</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Save
Comments to a File…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> writes the comments out on their
own, as XFDF, the interchange format readers agree on. The document is not in
the file. Sending back a marked-up copy of a contract means sending the
contract; sending an XFDF file means sending only your remarks, which is often
the difference between being allowed to comment and not. Anything drawn a
minute ago is written into the document first, so what goes out is what is on
screen.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guisubmenu>Mark Up</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Load
Comments from a File…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> reads such a file back and
adds what it holds. Anything referring to a page the document does not have is
skipped and reported rather than moved onto the last page, because a comment in
the wrong place is worse than one that is missing and said to be. XFDF is also
how comments travel between &pdfsmithy; and Acrobat without either having to
understand the other.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="page-keeping">
<title>Keeping what you have done</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Enter;</keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Keep the Changes on the
Page</guimenuitem></menuchoice> writes everything waiting into the document as
one undo step, and saving does it for you. The command is available exactly
when there is something to keep.</para>

<para>Things are written in a fixed order: what the page draws, then the words,
then the comments, then the form's layout, then the form's answers. Correcting
the words rewrites the page's instruction stream and renumbers its drawing
operations, so a move worked out against the old numbering has to be written
first; filling in a form touches only the field dictionaries and is independent
of both.</para>

<para>Anything that had to be refused, could not be made to fit, or was done
differently from what you asked is said at that point, in words. That last
category matters: a face whose licence says it may not travel with the file, or
an italic cut that is not installed so the upright one went in instead, is not
a refusal, because the change was made. But a document that silently ships
without the font it was set in is a document that looks wrong on the next
machine it opens on.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="properties">
<title>The Properties Sidebar</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F4</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Properties Sidebar</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
opens a panel beside the document which names the thing you last clicked
and lists its attributes. It holds no tools and no commands of its own, and it
is empty when nothing is selected, saying so: <quote>Nothing is selected. Click
a word, a form field, a comment or a picture in the document, and its
properties appear here.</quote></para>

<para>That is the point of it. A tool box asks you to remember which of twelve
panels holds the setting you want; a properties panel can be glanced at.
Editing happens in the document, and what belongs here are the attributes that
have no gesture on the page: a field's rules, a comment's author, an exact
point size. The moment something unrelated appears you have to start reading
the panel instead of glancing at it.</para>

<para>Several things can be selected at once, a picture on a page that also has
a line of text marked, and the panel shows whichever one you touched last,
because that is the one you are thinking about. It does not rebuild itself
while the focus is inside it, so typing into one of its boxes cannot take the
box out from under you mid-word.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>A line of text</term>
<listitem><para>The face the page sets it in and the resource name the page
gives that font, with whether the glyphs travel with the document; the typeface,
size, bold, italic and colour it is to be set in, all of which can be changed;
which page it is on; whether its box grows with the text or is kept with the
type narrowed into it; where it sits and how big it is in points; and what the
line said before you typed into it. A line that cannot be written into says why
here as well as on the page. See <xref linkend="page-correcting-format"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Something the page draws</term>
<listitem><para>Where it sits and how big it is, in points from the bottom left
corner of the page, both of which can be typed into as well as dragged; the
colour of the letters, or of a shape and its outline, and how thick that
outline is; a text object's font and size; how many pixels a picture holds and
the resolution that works out to, which keeps up while the picture is being
dragged; and where it comes in the page's painting order, which cannot be
changed yet and says so. A picture carries its own colours and takes no notice
of one set here. With more than one thing chosen, the alignment buttons appear
above the numbers.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A comment</term>
<listitem><para>What it says, its colour, how thick its line is, what kind of
mark it is, who made it, and which page it is on. All of it can be changed
here, and setting the page carries the comment to that page.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A form field being filled in</term>
<listitem><para>Its name, the label its author wrote beside it, its kind, the
page you are looking at it on, how many boxes on the paper it is made of,
whether the form insists on an answer, whether it locks the field, whether it
takes several lines, the choices a list offers, and the answer it holds now,
which keeps up as you type on the page. All of it is shown rather than changed:
a field's name and rules are the form author's, and the one thing that is
yours, the answer, is given on the page where you can see what you are
answering. The name can be selected and copied out, because that is what a
form's own instructions quote.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A form field being designed</term>
<listitem><para>Everything above, and all of it editable, plus how the field
looks and what it does. See <xref linkend="form-properties"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>A bookmark</term>
<listitem><para>What it says, which page it opens, whether it opens showing its
sub-entries, and the bold, italic and colour a reader will draw it in. See
<xref linkend="contents-properties"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Nothing in particular</term>
<listitem><para>With nothing chosen the panel describes the document itself,
and in the page grid it describes the sheets that are selected, so that it is
not an empty panel in the two modes where there is often nothing to
click.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="form-editing">
<title>Editing the Form</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F7</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit the Form</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
draws a form onto the page. A form is a layout before it is a data structure,
and a layout is designed by looking at it; a dialog full of coordinate spin
boxes asks the author to hold the page in their head while typing numbers about
it.</para>

<para>Only the field layer answers the mouse here. Laying a form out over a
page whose text also answers to the mouse is how a field ends up moving a
paragraph.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Forms</guimenu><guimenuitem>Build a
Form…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is the way in from the menu: it brings you
here with a text field already in hand and the properties sidebar open.</para>

<sect1 id="form-drawing">
<title>Drawing the fields</title>

<para>Pick a kind from the form toolbar or the <guimenu>Forms</guimenu> menu
and drag out the box it is to occupy. The kinds are:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Text Field</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A box to type into, on one line or several.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Tick Box</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>On or off, on its own.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Radio Group</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>One choice out of several. Consecutive drags join the same
group, because that is what <quote>yes, no, maybe</quote> is; changing the kind
away from radio and back again starts a second group.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Drop-Down</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A list that opens when it is clicked, and which can also accept
typing.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>List</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A list that stays open, and which can allow several entries to
be picked.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Push Button</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A button that does something: emptying the form, sending the
answers, jumping to a page or opening an address.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Signature Field</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>An empty place for a signature. Signing itself is a separate
job and cannot be done here.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>A click rather than a drag still places a field, at a sensible default
size for its kind, with its corner where the pointer was. The kind stays in
hand after a field is placed, because a form is laid out several boxes at a
time; <guimenuitem>Pick a Field</guimenuitem> puts it back. &Esc; first
disarms the kind and only then clears the choice, because <quote>stop doing
what I am about to do</quote> comes before <quote>forget what I
picked</quote>.</para>

<para>Move a field by dragging it, resize it by its eight handles, and hold
&Shift; on a corner to keep its proportions. The arrow keys nudge by a point,
and by ten with &Shift; held. &Del; marks the chosen fields for removal; they
are drawn crossed through until the changes are kept, because after that they
are not there at all. A drag on bare paper bands a selection, but only on a
page that already carries fields, so a document with no form never takes the
band away from the layer underneath. With two or more chosen, the alignment
buttons appear in the properties sidebar
(<xref linkend="page-objects-align"/>).</para>

<para>There is no handle to turn a field by. A widget's rectangle is stated as
two corners and cannot be written at an angle, so a turn handle would be an
offer the file format does not accept.</para>

<para>Field names are drawn inside the boxes where there is room, because three
fields called <quote>Date</quote> are what a form author spends the afternoon
telling apart. A field that has been drawn but not yet written into the
document is dashed.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="form-properties">
<title>What a field can be told</title>

<para>Everything else about a field is in the properties sidebar. The geometry
and the name appear with one field chosen; the rules appear when everything
chosen is of the same kind; the styling always does.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Where and what it is called</term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Place</guilabel> and <guilabel>Size</guilabel> in
points, <guilabel>Name</guilabel> (a full stop makes a group, so
<userinput>Address.Street</userinput> and <userinput>Address.Town</userinput>
become two fields under one heading) and <guilabel>Label</guilabel>, which is
what a reader shows beside the field and what a push button
says.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Rules</term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Kind</guilabel>, <guilabel>Required</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Read-only</guilabel>, and then whatever the kind allows:
<guilabel>Several lines</guilabel>, <guilabel>Hidden as it is typed</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Character limit</guilabel> and <guilabel>One box per letter</guilabel>
for text; <guilabel>Also accepts typing</guilabel> for a drop-down;
<guilabel>Several may be picked</guilabel> for a list;
<guilabel>Choices</guilabel>, one to a line, for both;
<guilabel>Ticked state</guilabel> for a tick box or a radio button, which is
the name that button's own drawing answers to; and <guilabel>Value</guilabel>,
what the field holds before anybody fills it in.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>How it looks</term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Font</guilabel> and its size (zero means
<quote>fit</quote>, leaving the size to whoever draws the field),
<guilabel>Ink</guilabel>, <guilabel>Background</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Border</guilabel> and <guilabel>Border style</guilabel> (solid,
dashed, raised, sunken or an underline only) with its width, and where the
<guilabel>Text</guilabel> sits in the box. Background and border have a
<guibutton>None</guibutton> button, because a form that states no background
and a form that states white are different documents and there has to be a way
back to neither.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Scripts</term>
<listitem><para>A field's formatting, keystroke, validation and calculation
scripts are shown as the document wrote them and are kept exactly as they are.
They cannot be edited here: the engine writes only the actions it generates
itself, and hand-written script is not something it can promise to write back.
The scripts are only shown when the field has any.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<note><para>A password box, a list that takes several answers and a drop-down
that also accepts typing are not among what is read back from a form, so those
three start unticked whatever the document says. Changing anything at all about
a field rewrites all of its flags, so tick them again here if the field had
them.</para></note>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="form-appearance">
<title>What the field appearance can and cannot promise</title>

<para>The page underneath is drawn without its fields, and this mode draws all
of them itself from the very description the engine will be handed. That is why
dragging a field does not leave a copy of it behind where the file still has
it: nothing was ever there.</para>

<para>What is drawn is a reconstruction from four things the file says about a
field: its colours, its border, its type and its alignment. It is exact for a
field &pdfsmithy; made, because the same four things are what it draws the
appearance from. It cannot be exact for a field whose author drew their own
appearance stream, a logo on a button or a bevel with a gradient in it, because
an appearance stream is a small program and this is not running it.</para>

<para>So the gesture is not the end of it. A third of a second after the last
change, the page and the work waiting on it are written to a scratch copy, the
copy is drawn with its fields, and the result is put back over the boxes. From
then on what is on the screen is the file.</para>

<para>Two further things about appearance are worth knowing before relying on
them. Every field is written with a border, a background and, for a tick box,
its tick, because a field with no drawing of its own is invisible in about half
the readers in use. And a field can only be set in one of the fourteen fonts
every reader already knows; a field in a font of your own would need that font
embedding, which is a separate job.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="form-refusals">
<title>What is refused, and when</title>

<para>All of these arrive while the mouse is still on the spot rather than at
saving time, when the work would already be done.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>A document carrying an XFA form is refused outright, the moment
a field kind is picked up or a field is drawn. XFA is a second and incompatible
description of the same fields; Acrobat honours it and everything else honours
the ordinary fields, so anything added would behave differently in every
reader.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A field with more than one box sharing its name keeps the
places its author gave it, so its box cannot be moved or resized from here.
A radio group is the ordinary case of this and its buttons are drawn plainly
with no handles. Its properties are still yours to change.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A field needs a name, and the name has to be free. A field can
be renamed but not moved into another group, because anything in the document
referring to it by name would be left pointing at nothing. A refused rename is
put back in the box rather than left on screen looking as though it
took.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A field waiting on a page that is then deleted is dropped and
said out loud, rather than written onto whichever page has moved into that
slot.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Formatting, validation and calculation are JavaScript, which is how every
&PDF; form does them, which PDF/A forbids, and which several readers do not run
at all. What a reader will not run, it shows as typed.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="form-behaviour">
<title>Behaviour, tab order and the answers</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Forms</guimenu><guimenuitem>Form Behaviour and
Answers…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> opens a window for the parts of a form that
are not a gesture on a page. It has five tabs.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>The Fields It Has</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Every field with its kind, page and place. Fields can be
renamed and removed here, and the box at the foot lists what building a form
cannot promise.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Tab Order</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The order the &Tab; key walks the fields of one page, set by
dragging the list into shape. Fields left out keep their place behind the ones
listed.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Behaviour</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How a value is shown (as typed, a number, an amount of money, a
percentage, a date, a time, a postcode or a telephone number), a range a number
must fall in, a value worked out from other fields (total, product, average,
smallest or largest), and what pressing a button does.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Answers</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Writing the answers out and reading them back (FDF, XFDF or
comma-separated values, chosen by the file name), gathering many filled-in
copies into one table with a row per document and a column per field, and
copying the fields from another document. That last needs the two to have the
same layout: the fields land at the coordinates they had, not next to whatever
text they were beside.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Place a Field</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The older way of putting a field on a page, by typing its four
numbers. Drawing it on the page is the ordinary way now, and this tab remains
for a field whose position is genuinely known as a
number.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>This window works on the file as it stood when it opened, and writes a
new file rather than changing the one in the window. It closes itself after a
successful write, because a second change made afterwards would be made to the
older file and would quietly drop what the first one did. See
<xref linkend="tools"/> for how all such windows behave.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="arranging">
<title>Arranging the Pages</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F8</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Arrange the Pages</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
puts every page of every file you have opened or inserted into one grid, in the
order the document will be saved in. It answers <quote>which pages, and in what
order</quote>. What the pages say is the other three modes' question, and one
view that tried to answer both would answer neither well.</para>

<para>It opens on a windowful of pages rather than on whatever size you last
read at, because rearranging a document is done by eye and needs a page's
neighbours on screen beside it. Set a size there yourself, with the status-bar
slider or with &Ctrl; and the wheel, and that is the size it keeps from then
on. Leaving puts the document back exactly as you left it: the same size, the
same spread or single column, and the same line.</para>

<para>Everything in this chapter is undoable, and each command counts as one
step however many pages it touched. Undo also covers what you keep on the page
in the other modes, and editing the bookmarks, because <quote>undo</quote>
meaning different things in different parts of one window is its own kind of
bug. The menu entry names the step it will undo.</para>

<sect1 id="arranging-selecting">
<title>Selecting and reordering</title>

<para>Click a page to select it, &Ctrl;-click to add a page to the selection,
&Shift;-click to select a run, and
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>A</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Select All</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to
take the lot. The status bar says how many are selected.</para>

<para>Drag a page to move it. A whole selection moves together, and it moves
between the pages of every file in the grid rather than only within the file it
came from, so a chapter from one document can be dropped into the middle of
another in one gesture. The drop position is shown as a line between thumbnails
while you drag. Dragging &PDF; files in from the file manager and dropping them
between two pages inserts them exactly there.</para>

<para>Almost everything works on the selection. Text recognition, compression,
watermarking, trimming, page numbering and exporting as images each offer the
choice between the pages you picked and the whole document, and start on the
selection when there is one. <quote>Make this one page searchable</quote> is a
normal thing to ask for. Two commands work differently and say so where they
are described: signing goes on the page you are looking at, or on every page
(<xref linkend="signing-placing"/>), and redaction acts on the pages you
actually marked (<xref linkend="redaction-marking"/>).</para>

<para>The right button opens a menu, and there is a different one in each mode,
because the mode is the whole of what a click means. On the page grid it offers
what can be done to whole pages. On the document it offers copying and marking
while reading, cutting and keeping while editing the page, and the field kinds
while editing the form. None of them offers to delete the page, which is the
page grid's question.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="arranging-turning">
<title>Turning, duplicating and deleting</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>L</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Rotate Left</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Turns the selected pages a quarter turn anticlockwise, or the
whole document when nothing is selected. Rotation is recorded as an attribute
of the page, so no image is resampled.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Rotate Right</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>The same, clockwise.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>D</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Duplicate Pages</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Puts a second copy of each selected page after it. The copy
refers to the same page in the same file, so duplicating a page costs no space
in the saved document.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut>&Del;</shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Delete Pages</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Removes the selected pages. The file on disk is untouched until
you save, and deleting every page asks first. The command works only where the
pages are on show: &Del; means something else on an open page, and one
keystroke removing the page being read, in a mode whose own tooltip promises
nothing can be changed by accident, is not a trade worth
making.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="arranging-inserting">
<title>Bringing more pages in</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>I</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Insert File…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
adds one or more documents to the one you already have. Because appending is
right only about half the time, and the other half you have a page selected
precisely because that is where the new material belongs, you are asked where
the pages should go: at the beginning, before the selected page, after it, or
at the end. Your answer is remembered for next time. Everything inserted in one
go is one undo step, however many files, and the new pages are selected
afterwards.</para>

<para>A locked file being inserted asks for its password, with the same
retrying as opening one, because a locked file being inserted is one you have
the password to, and refusing it in silence is how pages go missing from a
merge. Files taken from a network share are fetched all or nothing, for the
same reason.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Add Blank
Pages…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> asks how many and what size. The first choice
is <guilabel>Same as this document</guilabel>, and it is matched to the page
the new ones will sit beside rather than to page one, so a book with a
landscape plate in the middle gets another landscape sheet there rather than an
upright one. The named papers are offered as well, with an orientation, which
is disabled while the size is being matched, since offering to change it there
would be offering to break the match.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Import
Images…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> makes one page per picture and appends them,
at the picture's own size read at 300 &dpi;, which is what stops an A4 scan
arriving as a poster. With nothing open it starts a document instead.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Set Text as
Pages…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> makes pages out of typed or pasted text; see
<xref linkend="tools-typeset"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="arranging-extracting">
<title>Taking pages out</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>E</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Extract Pages to New File…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
writes the selected pages to a new document and leaves the one you are working
on exactly as it was. It is the command for <quote>I need pages 4 to 9 as their
own file</quote>. The copy carries the original's password and permissions: an
extract is the same content and has no business being the unprotected
one.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Split…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
breaks the whole document into several files, and offers the four questions
people actually ask:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>every so many pages, where one page per file gives one file per
invoice;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>at named ranges, where each comma-separated group such as
<userinput>1-3, 4-9, 10-last</userinput> becomes its own file;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>in pieces no larger than a size you give, for when the limit is
a mail server rather than a chapter boundary. The pieces are measured as they
are written, so the exact split is only known afterwards, and the dialog says
so instead of guessing;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>at each top-level bookmark, which for a manual or a scanned
book is usually the split you meant. The files can be named after the
bookmarks.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>The dialog lists the file names it is about to write, and how many pages
each will get, before anything is written. Writing twenty files is not
something anyone should have to do speculatively to find out what they get. The
document has to be on disk first, and you are asked to save if it is not.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="arranging-trimming">
<title>Trimming the edges</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Trim
Pages…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> takes the empty border off pages. Nothing is
cut away: trimming sets the visible area of the page, so the content is all
still there, readers show less of it, and <guilabel>Show the full pages
again</guilabel> puts it back at any time. A scan therefore keeps its exact
pixels, which cropping by re-rendering would not.</para>

<para>The page goes where the grid was and the four measurements go into a
panel at the edge of the window; drag the edges of the bright area on the page
and the numbers follow, or type them. The trim applies to every page in the
chosen range rather than only to the one on screen.</para>

<para><guibutton>Find the border for me</guibutton> renders a sample of the
pages and measures the white edge around them. It then offers the largest trim
that is safe for <emphasis>every</emphasis> page in the selection, because
trimming each page to its own content would produce a document whose pages are
all slightly different sizes, which looks worse than the margins did. Margins
are measured on the page as it is displayed, so <quote>left</quote> is your
left whatever the page's own rotation says.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="arranging-sheets">
<title>Several pages on one sheet</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Arrange on
Sheets…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> produces a <emphasis>file</emphasis> with 2,
4, 6, 8, 9 or 16 pages on each sheet. It is what you want for a handout, for a
reading copy that fits in a bag, and for anything that has to be sent rather
than printed; printing the same way is <xref linkend="printing"/>.</para>

<para>Nothing is rasterised. Each page is placed on the sheet whole, so the
text stays text and the sheets stay selectable and searchable; a four-up
handout of a scanned book is the same scan at the same resolution, arranged
differently. You can set the border around the sheet and the gap between pages,
draw a thin line around each page, and fill the grid downwards instead of in
reading order. A preview beside the settings shows the arrangement, and a line
under it says how many pages go across and down.</para>

<para>This is the one command here that changes how many pages the document
has. Undo puts all of them back, not half of them.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="contents">
<title>Contents and Bookmarks</title>

<para>A document's table of contents is the thing that makes a three-hundred-page
manual usable.
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>B</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Contents Sidebar</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
shows it at the side of the window. It stays closed until you ask for it: most
documents have bookmarks, so opening it by itself cost a third of the window to
a panel nobody had asked for, and closing it did no good because the next file
opened it again.</para>

<sect1 id="contents-navigating">
<title>Getting about</title>

<para>The sidebar lists the entries as a tree, each with the page it points at
in a column of its own. Click one to go and read that page. It also follows you
the other way round: as you move through the document, the entry covering the
current page is highlighted, so you can always see where in the document you
are.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="contents-editing">
<title>Editing the contents</title>

<para>The sidebar edits the table of contents as well as showing it, and every
change goes onto the same undo stack as everything else.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Renaming</term>
<listitem><para>Double-click an entry and type. A single click goes to the
page, which is the thing done a hundred times a session; renaming is the thing
done once.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Adding</term>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Add for Page …</guimenuitem> in the &RMB; menu
names the page now showing. The new entry appears beside whatever you had
selected rather than at the very end, because a bookmark added while a chapter
is selected belongs next to it, and goes straight into renaming, because nobody
wants a bookmark called <quote>New bookmark</quote>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Re-nesting</term>
<listitem><para>Drag an entry onto another to make it a sub-entry, or between
two to move it among them; the whole subtree travels with it. An entry dropped
into a closed one would appear to have vanished, so its new parents are opened,
and recorded as open, since that is what the file will be asked to save. The
&RMB; menu does the same by keystrokes: <guimenuitem>Move Up</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Move Down</guimenuitem> among its siblings,
<guimenuitem>Make a Sub-entry</guimenuitem> to put it under the entry above it,
and <guimenuitem>Move Out One Level</guimenuitem> to bring it back out. There
are also <guimenuitem>Expand All</guimenuitem> and <guimenuitem>Collapse
All</guimenuitem>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Deleting</term>
<listitem><para>&Del;, or <guimenuitem>Delete</guimenuitem>. An entry with
sub-entries under it raises the one question that matters:
<guibutton>Keep Them</guibutton> lifts them into the hole their parent leaves,
in their own order and at their own place in the document, while
<guibutton>Delete Them Too</guibutton> takes the whole chapter. The question
comes before anything is changed, so cancelling costs nothing. Taking the
sub-entries silently is one of the two things you might have meant and never
the one you expected.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Opening and closing an entry to read the tree is deliberately not an undo
step: an undo stack full of <quote>opened a chapter</quote> is an undo stack
nobody dares press. What the saved file says about an entry being open is set
on purpose in the properties sidebar instead, and a triangle turned in passing
travels along with the next real change.</para>

<para>A document with no table of contents shows a hint and two buttons rather
than an empty panel. <guibutton>Add a Bookmark for This Page</guibutton> starts
the tree by hand. <guibutton>Build From Headings</guibutton> makes an entry out
of every paragraph set larger than the text around it, which is a guess and is
said to be one both before and afterwards, because nothing in a &PDF; records
which paragraph is a heading. A document set in one size throughout, or one
whose words are part of a scanned picture, offers nothing to go on and is told
so plainly. Read what it built before saving.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="contents-properties">
<title>What an entry can say</title>

<para>A bookmark has no appearance on the page to click, so its attributes live
in the properties sidebar (<xref linkend="properties"/>) rather than behind a
dialog. Selecting an entry shows what it says, which page it opens
(<guilabel>None</guilabel> is allowed, and makes it a heading for the entries
under it rather than a place to go), whether it opens showing its sub-entries,
and the bold, italic and colour a reader will draw it in.
<guibutton>Default</guibutton> beside the colour hands that decision back to
the reader.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="contents-survives">
<title>Bookmarks follow their page</title>

<para>While a document is open, a bookmark is anchored to the page itself
rather than to its number. Reordering pages, deleting them or inserting a file
therefore moves each bookmark with its page instead of leaving it pointing at
whatever now happens to sit at that number. An entry whose page has left the
document altogether is marked as such rather than quietly retargeted, and
entries pointing outside the document are dropped when it is written, because a
bookmark that goes nowhere is worse than one that is missing.</para>

<para>The table of contents also survives saving. That is worth stating because
it is not a given: a document editor that loses a manual's navigation the first
time somebody rotates a page is not a document editor.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="scans">
<title>Scans</title>

<para>A scanned page is a picture of text. It cannot be searched, selected,
copied or indexed, which is why a scanned contract is so much less useful than
the same contract as a document.
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Recognise Text (OCR)…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
fixes that.</para>

<sect1 id="scans-recognition">
<title>Text recognition</title>

<para>The page itself is never re-rendered. Each page is rasterised only in
order to be read, and the words that were recognised are then written back over
the picture as an <emphasis>invisible</emphasis> text layer, positioned over
the words you can see. The page looks exactly as it did, a scan that went in at
600 &dpi; comes out with the very same image bytes, and it suddenly selects,
copies, searches and indexes.</para>

<para>Pick the languages of the document under <guilabel>Languages</guilabel>.
Only the languages Tesseract has data files for on this machine are offered,
under their proper names, with the one matching your desktop language ticked to
start with; if the one you need is missing, install its package.
<literal>tesseract-ocr-deu</literal> and <literal>tesseract-ocr-eng</literal>
are the usual two. <guilabel>Detail</guilabel> chooses the resolution the page
is read at: 200 &dpi; is fastest, 300 &dpi; is the sensible default, and 400 or
600 &dpi; are for small print and poor scans respectively, at the cost of
time.</para>

<para><guilabel>Skip pages that already have text</guilabel> is on by default
and should stay on. Running recognition over a page that was generated
digitally adds a second, slightly wrong text layer on top of a perfectly good
one, which is the classic way to ruin a searchable document.</para>

<para>Recognition runs page by page behind a progress window that can be
cancelled; cancelling stops at the next page boundary and leaves the document
as it was.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="scans-straightening">
<title>Straightening</title>

<para><guilabel>Straighten crooked pages</guilabel> measures how far the page
is tilted and corrects it with a transformation wrapped around the page's
existing content. There is no resampling, so nothing softens and no detail is
lost; the report afterwards says how many pages were straightened and what the
worst tilt was.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="scans-cleanup">
<title>Helping the recognition along</title>

<para>Two settings under <guilabel>Help the recognition along</guilabel> deal
with bad originals. <guilabel>Ignore specks up to</guilabel> discards specks
smaller than the given number of pixels: dust and scanner noise that Tesseract
would otherwise try to read as punctuation. <guilabel>Even out uneven
lighting</guilabel> is for a page that was photographed rather than scanned,
bright in the middle and dim at the edges, which Tesseract reads far worse at
the edges than it needs to.</para>

<para>Both affect only the image that is handed to the recognition. Neither
touches the page: cleaning up the page itself would mean re-encoding it, and
&pdfsmithy; does not re-encode pages.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="scans-unavailable">
<title>When it is not available</title>

<para>Text recognition is done by Tesseract, with Leptonica cleaning up the
images it is given. A build made on a machine without them has no recognition
at all and says so. A build that has them but finds no language data installed
says that instead, and names the kind of package to install.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="signing">
<title>Signing and Stamping</title>

<para>Signatures, watermarks and page numbers are the same operation
underneath: something is embedded as a resource and then placed on top of the
page. The page's own content is never decoded or re-encoded, so a signed scan
is byte for byte the scan it was, plus a signature.</para>

<sect1 id="signing-signature">
<title>Capturing a signature</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>G</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Sign…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> puts the
page where the grid was and the signing tools in a panel beside it. Signing is
a repeated act, so a signature is captured once and then kept for every
document afterwards. There are two ways to capture one:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guibutton>From File…</guibutton> reads a photograph or a scan
of your signature on paper. A photograph taken with a phone is
fine.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Draw…</guibutton> lets you sign in a box with the
mouse, a trackpad or a pen tablet.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Either way you are asked to name it, so that a list of three signatures
is a list of three names.</para>

<para>A photographed or scanned signature arrives as dark strokes on light
paper, and stamped as it is it would cover the document with a white box that
looks obviously pasted on. The paper is therefore made transparent
automatically: pixels brighter than a threshold become fully transparent and
the ones a little below it partly so, which keeps the edges of the strokes soft
rather than jagged, and the fully transparent margin is cropped away so that
the stamp is all signature.</para>

<para>Your signatures are kept as image files with transparency in the
application's own data directory. They never leave the machine, and they are
deliberately kept as files rather than inside the configuration so that you can
find, back up and delete them yourself; <guibutton>Remove</guibutton> in the
panel deletes one from this machine, after asking.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="signing-placing">
<title>Placing it</title>

<para>Pick a signature from the list and the page appears beside it. Drag the
signature to where it belongs, or click anywhere on the page to put it there,
and pull the corner handle to resize it; the <guilabel>Size</guilabel> slider
does the same thing numerically, as a proportion of the page width.</para>

<para>The signature goes on the page you were looking at.
<guilabel>Put it on every page</guilabel> stamps the same signature at the same
coordinates on every page of the document, for initialling a contract, which is
otherwise thirty separate acts.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="signing-not-cryptographic">
<title>What this is not</title>

<important><para>This is a picture of your signature placed on the page. It is
not a cryptographic signature: the document is not sealed, nothing about it can
be checked afterwards, and a reader will not report it as signed. It is the
electronic equivalent of signing a printout, which is what most forms and most
contracts actually ask for, and it should not be relied on where a verifiable
signature is the point. A signature <emphasis>field</emphasis> drawn onto a
form (<xref linkend="form-drawing"/>) is likewise an empty place for a
signature rather than a signature.</para></important>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="signing-watermark">
<title>Watermarks</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Watermark…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
lays text or a picture across the selected pages. <guilabel>Draft</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Confidential</guilabel>, <guilabel>Copy</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Sample</guilabel> and <guilabel>Do not copy</guilabel> are there as
presets, and you can type anything else. Size, colour, how faint it is and the
angle are yours to set, and <guilabel>Outline only, so the page stays
readable</guilabel> draws the letters hollow, which is worth using on anything
anyone still has to read. A picture, such as a logo or a departmental stamp,
can be used instead of text, anchored to the centre or to any corner, at a
width you give as a share of the page.</para>

<para>The preview is the page itself with the stamp drawn on it the way it will
be written, so turn to a busy page to check that it stays readable through the
watermark. Judging a watermark against a white rectangle means applying it,
looking, undoing and starting again.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="signing-numbering">
<title>Page numbers, headers and Bates stamping</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Page
Numbers…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> stamps a short line of text into a corner
of every selected page. The text is built from placeholders:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><literal>{page}</literal></term>
<listitem><para>the running number.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><literal>{pages}</literal></term>
<listitem><para>how many pages the run covers.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><literal>{bates}</literal></term>
<listitem><para>the Bates number, with its prefix and zero padding
applied.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><literal>{file}</literal></term>
<listitem><para>the document's file name.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><literal>{date}</literal></term>
<listitem><para>today's date, in your short format.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Presets cover the usual shapes, and the page shows the stamp where it
will land as you change the settings, with a line under it reading what this
page will say. Turn to a page outside the run and it says that instead of
showing you nothing. You choose the corner, the type size, how far in from the
edge it sits, its colour, and the number the first stamped page is called,
which is what it is called rather than which page it is.</para>

<para>Bates stamping is the same mechanism with a prefix and zero padding, so
that every page of a bundle carries a unique, sequential, quotable identifier
such as <literal>ACME-000147</literal>. Legal and audit work needs that, and
doing it by hand across a four-hundred-page disclosure is how mistakes
happen.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="redaction">
<title>Redaction</title>

<para>A black rectangle drawn over a name is not redaction. The name is still
in the file, and anybody who selects the text, copies it, or opens the document
in an editor has it. That mistake has ended careers and lost court cases, and
it is made most often by people who believed they had removed something.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Redact…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
does the only thing that counts: it takes the content out of the page's
instruction stream, and only then paints the black box. The box is cosmetic.
The content is gone whether or not anyone looks at the result.</para>

<sect1 id="redaction-marking">
<title>Marking what has to go</title>

<para>The page goes where the grid was and the tools into a panel at the edge
of the window. It moves between pages itself, because a redaction usually spans
several and having to start again on each one would guarantee a missed
page.</para>

<para>Drag across anything that has to go. Click a box to select it, and
<guibutton>Remove</guibutton> or <guibutton>Remove All</guibutton> to unmark.
<guilabel>Marked areas</guilabel> lists them with their page and size, and,
this is the part worth reading, <guilabel>Text that will be destroyed</guilabel>
shows the actual words that your marks will remove, so that a box drawn two
millimetres too far left is caught before it takes a word you needed. On a scan
there is nothing to list and the panel says so: the picture itself is edited
instead, and any recognised text over it goes with it.</para>

<para>Within the session, undo puts the untouched pages back. Once the file has
been written, though, the content is not in it any more and cannot be recovered
from it, which is the point, and which the panel states plainly, under
<guilabel>What this does and does not do</guilabel>, before you press
<guibutton>Redact</guibutton>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="redaction-text">
<title>How text is removed</title>

<para>The page's instruction stream is walked operator by operator, tracking
the matrices that decide where anything lands, so that every glyph can be given
a box of its own. Glyphs whose box touches a marked area are dropped, and each
one is replaced by the equivalent spacing, so that everything which survives
stays exactly where it was.</para>

<para>The removal is per character rather than per instruction because a whole
line of text is usually drawn by a single operation: throwing that operation
away would take the rest of the line with it. Removing a name from a line
therefore leaves the rest of the sentence intact and correctly spaced.</para>

<para>Where the width of a character is not stated by the document's own font
tables, it is estimated generously, and the estimate errs deliberately towards
removing too much. An over-wide estimate deletes a character more than you
asked for; an under-wide one leaves half a name behind. Only one of those two
failures matters.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="redaction-images">
<title>How pictures are removed</title>

<para>A picture that overlaps a marked area is decoded, the area is painted out
in its pixels, and the result is embedded again under a new name, so a second
placement of the same picture elsewhere on the page keeps its own pixels rather
than losing them to your mark.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="redaction-limits">
<title>What it cannot promise</title>

<para>The panel states these limits, and so does this handbook, because a
redaction tool that is vague about its edges is worse than none.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Text drawn inside an embedded drawing is not searched character
by character. When such a drawing overlaps a marked area, the whole drawing is
removed instead.</para></listitem>

<listitem><para>Fax pictures (CCITT and JBIG2) and JPEG 2000 pictures cannot be
edited in place. Rather than choose silently between destroying the page and
not redacting it, the page is flattened to an image, which removes the marked
content but costs that page its selectable text. You are told afterwards, in
words, which pages this happened to.</para></listitem>

<listitem><para>Attachments, and text hidden in the document's own properties,
are not touched. Use
<menuchoice><guimenu>Document</guimenu><guimenuitem>Clean Up
Document…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> for those
(<xref linkend="document-cleanup"/>).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Afterwards you are told how many areas were dealt with, and, separately
and in words rather than in a status line, what else you should know before
trusting the result. Anything the program had to compromise on is said out loud
rather than left for you to discover in the published file.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="smaller">
<title>Making Documents Smaller</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Pages</guimenu><guimenuitem>Make
Smaller…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> offers four levels. The first cannot lose
anything; the other three trade picture quality for size, and cannot be undone
by compressing again, though they can be undone in the window.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Lossless (nothing is re-encoded)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Structural packing only: object streams, re-compressed streams,
duplicate objects merged. Every pixel survives. On a text document the saving
is typically somewhere between 5 and 30 percent; on a scan it is close to
nothing, because the bulk of a scan is its pictures and this level does not
touch them.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Balanced (good for reading and printing)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Images are re-encoded at 150 &dpi;. Comfortable on screen and
still prints acceptably. This is the default.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Strong (good for email)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Images at 110 &dpi;. The level to reach for when something has
to fit under an attachment limit.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Smallest (visibly softer images)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Images at 72 &dpi;. As small as is still useful, and it looks
it.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para><guilabel>Convert to grayscale</guilabel> often halves a colour scan and
does nothing at all to a document that was black text on white to begin with.
Monochrome images are treated far more gently than the level suggests, since
downsampling a black-and-white scan to 110 &dpi; would destroy it.</para>

<para>The three lossy levels need Ghostscript, which does the image
downsampling. It is run as a separate program and is an optional dependency:
without it, the lossless level still works and the dialog says why the others
are unavailable. Install the <literal>ghostscript</literal> package to get
them.</para>

<sect1 id="smaller-estimate">
<title>Asking first</title>

<para><guibutton>How much would this save?</guibutton> answers the question
before you commit to it. A handful of pages are compressed for real and the
ratio is applied to the rest, so you are told roughly what the document would
come down to in a second or two rather than after compressing a
four-hundred-page scan to find out whether compressing it was worth doing. The
result says how many pages it was measured on, and calls itself a rough guess
when that number was very small. A number presented as exact when it came from
four pages would be a small lie repeated on every document.</para>

<para>Afterwards, the report says what was actually achieved. It also says when
nothing was: scans are very often already stored below the target resolution,
in which case no level will help. The result is only kept when it is genuinely
smaller than the original, and when it is not, nothing is changed and no undo
step is made.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="document">
<title>The Document as a Whole</title>

<sect1 id="document-properties">
<title>Properties</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Document</guimenu><guimenuitem>Document
Properties…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> holds the title, author, subject and
keywords, what the document was created with, what wrote it, and the two dates.
An empty date reads <guilabel>not recorded</guilabel> rather than showing a
made-up one. Emptying a field removes it from the document entirely.</para>

<para>The producer, <guilabel>Written by</guilabel>, is kept as it was rather
than overwritten on save, so that saving a document does not announce which
tool you used.</para>

<para>Changing the properties is an ordinary undo step and is written on the
next save.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="document-cleanup">
<title>Cleaning up</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Document</guimenu><guimenuitem>Clean Up
Document…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> begins by naming what the document
actually carries: the author, the title, what it was created with and when, an
XMP metadata packet, JavaScript that runs when the document opens, and how many
files are embedded in it. <quote>Author: A. Person</quote> is a fact somebody
can act on; <quote>metadata</quote> is not. A document with nothing worth
removing is told so.</para>

<para>Two things can then be removed, both ticked by default: the document
information, and JavaScript, attachments and link actions. The pages themselves
are not touched, and nothing changes on disk until you save.</para>

<para>What is listed is read from the file on disk, so save first if you want
the inventory to include something you have added in this session.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="document-password">
<title>Protection and passwords</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Document</guimenu><guimenuitem>Password…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
sets or removes the password that opens the document.
<guilabel>Remove the password</guilabel> is available only when there is one.
The document has to have been saved once before it can be protected.</para>

<para>Encryption is AES-256. Nothing weaker is offered, because the older
schemes in the &PDF; specification are broken.</para>

<para>Two permissions can be set alongside it: <guilabel>Ask readers not to
print</guilabel> and <guilabel>Ask readers not to copy text</guilabel>. The
dialog says what they are worth, and so does this handbook: permission settings
are a request, not a lock. Any reader may ignore them, so the password is what
actually protects the document.</para>

<para>Unlike almost everything else in the program, this is not an undo step:
the document is saved immediately, because a password the window knows about
and the file does not is a password the next
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo> would write
straight over. The dialog says plainly that a document whose password is lost
cannot be opened again, by this program or by any other.</para>

<para>The protection a document was opened with is carried into every save,
including into pages extracted out of it, so a plain save never quietly unlocks
a file its owner had locked. Opening a different document forgets it.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="document-compare">
<title>Comparing two documents</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Document</guimenu><guimenuitem>Compare
with…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> answers the question behind nearly every
comparison of two &PDF; files: is this still the contract I read? The
comparison works on two files on disk, so you are offered the chance to save
first.</para>

<para>An honest answer needs two witnesses, so both are used. The extracted
text finds the changes that matter to a reader, a name, a figure, a negation,
and says which words went and which arrived. The rendered pixels find
everything text cannot see: a swapped signature image, a redrawn table, a font
substitution that reflowed a page without altering a single word. A page whose
words match but whose appearance does not gets a verdict of its own and says
how much of the page differs, because that is exactly the case a reader is
least likely to catch unaided.</para>

<para>The words are compared as a sequence, never as a set. The distinction is
not academic: <quote>the tenant shall not sublet the property</quote> and
<quote>the tenant shall sublet the property not</quote> hold precisely the same
words, and any comparison built on sets or word counts declares them
equal.</para>

<para>Two limits are worth knowing. Pages are paired by position, which is
right for the ordinary case of one document saved twice and wrong for a page
inserted in the middle: that shifts everything after it, and the whole tail is
reported as changed. And a scan carries no text, so two scans are judged on
their pixels alone; nothing can tell whether a picture of a sentence says
something different without reading it.</para>

<para>The window lists the changed pages, shows the two versions side by side
with the differences marked, and names the words that went and the words that
arrived. Two identical documents are told so and no window opens.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="document-archiving">
<title>Archiving</title>

<para>PDF/A is the shape a document has to be in before an archive will take
it. The standard is mostly a list of things a file may not do: no encryption,
no scripts, no dependency on anything outside itself, and above all no font it
does not carry, because a document that borrows Helvetica from the reader's
machine stops looking like itself the moment that machine is gone.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guisubmenu>Export as</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Save
for Archiving…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> writes a copy as PDF/A-2b, which is
what most archives ask for today. It writes the document as it stands in the
window, including changes you have not saved. The other two levels, PDF/A-1b
(the oldest and strictest, no transparency and no layers, accepted everywhere)
and PDF/A-3b (as 2b, but a file may be carried along inside the document), are
written from the command line (<xref linkend="command-line"/>).</para>

<para>The conversion is done by Ghostscript, run as a separate program, so it
is an optional dependency; without it installed the command says so and points
at the <literal>ghostscript</literal> package.</para>

<para>Everything the conversion changed and everything it could not fix is
reported afterwards, in words, because both belong in front of somebody about
to hand a file to an archive. The usual things it will tell you are that the
password protection was removed because PDF/A does not allow it, that scripts
and actions which run on their own were removed, that the files carried inside
the document were dropped (convert to PDF/A-3 if they matter), that the fonts
were embedded, and which colour profile was used.</para>

<para>The other half of archiving is inspection, and it is deliberately called
a look rather than a validation.
<userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> archive-check
<replaceable>file.pdf</replaceable></userinput> finds the handful of faults
that account for most rejections: encryption, scripts, attachments, fonts that
are not embedded, a missing output intent. A file can pass every check here and
still be refused by a strict validator. A real PDF/A validator is a substantial
piece of software; veraPDF is the reference one, and it is what to reach for
when an archive has to sign the file off. The check also reads only what the
file says about itself, so a document claiming to be PDF/A-2b is taken at its
word on that point.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="document-exports">
<title>Everything else the engine can write</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guisubmenu>Export as</guisubmenu></menuchoice>
gathers the eight things the program can write that are not this document as it
stands. They are one menu rather than eight entries in the File menu because
they are eight answers to one question, and <quote>Export as</quote> is where
somebody with that question looks. None of them changes the document in the
window.</para>

<sect2 id="export-images">
<title>Pages as pictures</title>

<para><guimenuitem>Export as Images…</guimenuitem> writes one picture per page
into a directory you choose. <guilabel>Format</guilabel> offers PNG (sharp,
larger files), JPEG (smaller, for photographs) and TIFF (for print workflows);
<guilabel>Detail</guilabel> offers 96 &dpi; for the screen, 150 for every day,
300 for print and 600 for archival use. <guilabel>Quality</guilabel> goes quiet
for PNG rather than sitting there implying it does something to a lossless
format. It acts on the selected pages or on the whole document. Any page that
could not be drawn is counted and reported, since nothing else on screen
distinguishes eight images from the two of them that worked.</para>

<para><guimenuitem>Import Images…</guimenuitem> is the way back;
see <xref linkend="arranging-inserting"/>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="export-text">
<title>Text, HTML, Markdown and tables</title>

<para><guimenuitem>Plain Text…</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>HTML…</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Markdown…</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Tables as a Spreadsheet…</guimenuitem> write the document's words
out. Every one of them says the same thing afterwards, every time, because it
is true every time: a &PDF; holds letters at positions rather than sentences,
so what came out was worked out from the layout, and it is worth reading over
before relying on it.</para>

<para>The reconstruction is good and it guesses in known places. Reading order
is worked out by cutting the page into blocks, so a layout with the columns
interleaved by hand, or one whose gutter is filled by a picture, comes out in
the wrong order. Which paragraph is a heading, which line is a list item and
which words are emphasised are guessed from size, from leading characters and
from the font's own name. A word broken by a hyphen at the end of a line is
joined back together when the next line starts in lower case, which is wrong
for a compound whose hyphen was real. A table is read column by column by the
plain-text export, because its columns look exactly like a page's columns; use
the table export for tables. Text that is part of a scanned picture is not text
and does not come out at all, so run the page through text recognition first
(<xref linkend="scans"/>).</para>

<para>Table detection needs every row to have the same number of cells, so a
table with cells left empty or merged across columns is found in pieces or not
at all, and the confidence it reports is a judgement about how table-like the
geometry is rather than a probability. HTML carries pictures across only where
the page draws them itself and they are in a format that can be decoded here;
fax and JPEG 2000 pictures, and pictures drawn inside an embedded drawing, are
left out.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="export-web">
<title>For the web and for a printing press</title>

<para><guimenuitem>Save for the Web…</guimenuitem> writes a copy laid out so
that a reader on a slow connection can show page one before it has the rest.
It is worth almost nothing on a local disk and a great deal on a slow link,
which is the only reason the format has the feature. It is a separate file
rather than a way of saving this one: how the bytes are laid out for somebody
downloading them is no business of the copy you are working on.</para>

<para><guimenuitem>Save for Commercial Printing…</guimenuitem> writes PDF/X-1a,
which is what most printers still ask for. The other levels are a choice for
somebody who already knows they need one, and the command line is where that
somebody is. Ghostscript does the conversion, so the command says plainly when
it is not installed rather than writing something that only looks like PDF/X.
An output intent for print has to be a CMYK profile describing a real printing
condition; if none is found on this system that is reported rather than guessed
around, because guessing a printing condition is how a job comes back the wrong
colour. This is a conversion and not a preflight check: a file that comes out
of it still has to pass whatever your printer runs, which is what
<xref linkend="tools-preflight"/> is for.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="printing">
<title>Printing</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>P</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Print</guimenuitem></menuchoice> and
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Print Preview</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
open the usual print dialog with an extra tab, <guilabel>Pages &amp;
Layout</guilabel>. Most viewers hand the file to the printing system and call
that printing, which works until you want two pages on a sheet, or a booklet,
or only one side of the paper, which are the things people actually print for.
Here the imposition is done first and the printer is only ever given finished
sheets.</para>

<para>The controls live inside the system dialog rather than in a second window
in front of it, so that Cancel really cancels everything.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Pages</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Empty prints everything. Otherwise a range such as
<userinput>1-4, 8, 12-last</userinput>; <userinput>odd</userinput> and
<userinput>even</userinput> are accepted too, the order you write is the order
that is printed, and a repeated page is printed repeatedly. Odd and even mean
the position in the job rather than the page number in the file, which is what
makes manual duplex line up after the stack is turned
over.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Layout</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>One page per sheet, or 2, 4, 6, 9 or 16 of them, or
<guilabel>Booklet (fold in the middle)</guilabel>, which orders the pages so
that the stack folds and staples into a booklet that reads correctly. A booklet
is padded to a multiple of four; booklets and two-up use landscape
sheets.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Size</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Shrink oversized pages</guilabel> only ever shrinks,
so small pages keep their size; <guilabel>Fit to the paper</guilabel> also
enlarges; <guilabel>Actual size</guilabel> prints exact physical dimensions and
may clip; <guilabel>Custom scale</guilabel> takes a
percentage.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Sides</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Every page, front sides only, or back sides only: the two
halves of manual duplex on a printer that cannot turn the paper over itself.
Print the front sides, put the stack back in, print the back
sides.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Extra margin</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Millimetres of white kept around the printable area, for a
printer whose margins are tighter than it admits.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Three further switches: <guilabel>Print back to front</guilabel> for
printers that stack face up, <guilabel>Print in grayscale</guilabel>, and
<guilabel>Draw a line around each page</guilabel>, which is useful as a cut
mark when several pages share a sheet. A line in the tab says how many sheets
of paper the job will take before you commit to it, and says so instead if the
range cannot be read.</para>

<para>The preview runs the same imposition, so what is on screen is what will
come out, down to booklet order and blank slots. A preview of the source
document instead of the print job would be worse than none. It has no settings
of its own and uses whatever the print dialog was last set to; settings are
remembered even when the dialog is cancelled, because you have said what
you want and the preview should show that.</para>

<para>Pages are rasterised for printing, but at exactly the size they are drawn
at rather than at a fixed resolution and scaled afterwards, which is what makes
printed output look soft.</para>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="tools">
<title>The Tools</title>

<para>The <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> menu holds the windows that look
<emphasis>into</emphasis> a document: what colour it uses, which fonts it
carries, how big its pictures really are, what its page boxes say, and whether
a printer or an archive will accept it. Two entries in it, and two in the
<guimenu>Forms</guimenu> menu, are not windows at all but ways into the editing
modes, and they are listed here too so that the whole menu is accounted
for.</para>

<sect1 id="tools-how">
<title>How the windows behave</title>

<para>All of them read a <emphasis>file</emphasis> rather than the list of
pages in the window, so the document is saved first if it has unsaved changes,
and you are asked before that happens. Anything waiting on the page is written
into the document before the save, so a comment drawn a minute ago is in the
file the tool reads.</para>

<para>Each of them writes a <emphasis>new</emphasis> file beside the original,
tells you what it actually did, and offers <guibutton>Open the
Result</guibutton> or <guibutton>Leave It on Disk</guibutton>. None of these
operations is one to have applied silently to the document you are working on.
The consequence is that they are not undo steps: what they produce is a
different file, and the way back is the file you still have.</para>

<para>Three of them close themselves after a successful write, because
everything in them was worked out against the file as it stood when they
opened, and a second change made afterwards would be addressed to a document
the first one has already superseded.</para>

<para>Every one of these windows has a box or a button saying what it cannot
promise. It is not an apology: <quote>no problems found</quote> means nothing
unless you also know what nobody looked at.</para>

<para>All of them are greyed out with nothing open, except
<guimenuitem>Set Text as Pages…</guimenuitem>, which makes a document out of
text and is the one thing here worth reaching for with an empty
window.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-preflight">
<title>Checking the document</title>

<para><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>P</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Check the Document…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
runs the document against a profile, a list of rules with a severity each, and
reports what it found, grouped so that anything which would get the file
rejected is at the top. The profiles cover the PDF/A levels, the PDF/X levels
and a general print-ready one, and a profile saved in a file can be loaded.
Problems that can be mended are ticked and fixed in one pass, and the report
can be written out as a PDF to hand to a printer.</para>

<para>The box headed <guilabel>What was not checked</guilabel> is the most
important part of the window. A profile may name rules this program cannot
judge; those are listed there rather than quietly left out. Ink outside the
trim box is not measured, only what is drawn on the pages themselves is
examined (so a font used only inside an annotation's appearance is not seen),
font licences can be read only for OpenType and TrueType, a picture that is
never drawn is not judged for resolution, emptiness is decided from drawing
instructions so white on white counts as painted, and tagging is checked only
for presence and missing alternative text, because reading order is a judgement
no program makes well.</para>

<para>This is a preflight check and not a certification. A file that passes
here can still be refused by a strict validator.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-colour">
<title>Colour</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Colour…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
reads what the document does with colour, which spaces it uses, which spot inks
it needs a separate plate for, whether it carries an ICC profile or an output
intent, and how thin its thinnest stroke is, and then changes it. It can also
measure the ink coverage, page by page, and report the highest total it found,
which is what tells a printer whether a page will dry.</para>

<para>Converting to greyscale, black and white, RGB or CMYK rewrites the colour
operators of text and vector art <emphasis>in place</emphasis>, which is exact:
every byte that is not a colour comes out untouched. Only pictures have their
pixels converted. Where a colour-managed conversion into CMYK is asked for and
this build cannot do it itself, the window says so plainly and hands the whole
document to Ghostscript instead, which means the page selection cannot be
honoured on that route, rather than applying some arithmetic and calling the
result a conversion.</para>

<para>The other tabs replace one colour with another throughout, rename or
merge spot inks (a printer plates by name, so pointing two inks at one new name
is how they are merged onto one plate), dissolve a spot ink into process colour
through the tint transform the document already carries, thicken hairlines so
they survive the press, set black to overprint or to knock out, and show one
ink on its own as a plate, which looks like the plate rather than like the page
and is the point of it.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-fonts">
<title>Fonts</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Fonts…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
lists every font the document uses, whether it travels with the file, whether
only part of it is there, and whether its text can be copied out and searched.
Most of the questions people actually have about a &PDF; are font questions in
disguise: why is this eleven megabytes, why can nobody copy the text, why does
the printer say Helvetica is missing, why does the same typeface appear four
times.</para>

<para>The repairs follow from the reading. It embeds the faces the document is
missing without rewriting a single page, so every character code keeps its
meaning and every width the document states is kept, which is what stops the
text reflowing; left to itself it picks a metrically compatible stand-in.
It writes the tables that make text copyable, joins duplicate copies of one
font, cuts embedded fonts down to the glyphs actually used, throws the glyphs
of a standard face away where the reference is enough, and replaces one font
with another.</para>

<para>The last tab adds a font to the document, with the characters you name.
That is what lifts the one real limit on correcting text
(<xref linkend="page-correcting-refusals"/>): a document that never contained
an umlaut cannot be made to say a word with one in the font it carries, and
this is how the umlaut gets in. At most 255 characters can be added at a time,
and nothing outside the basic multilingual plane, which rules out most emoji
and the rarer Chinese characters.</para>

<para>Some of this cannot be done honestly, and the window says which. A
composite font's character codes are glyph numbers meaningful only inside the
font programme that is missing, so nothing can be put behind them; a font whose
codes the document never explains cannot be given a substitute with the same
meaning; and a Type 3 font draws its letters with the document's own drawing
operators rather than carrying a font programme at all, so there is nothing to
supply and no substitute that would mean the same thing. Those are named rather
than guessed at. Font licences are reported and never enforced.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-pictures">
<title>Pictures</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Pictures…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
shows every picture in the document with the number that actually matters, its
effective resolution on the page rather than its pixel count, alongside its
colour space, its compression, how many bytes it takes and how often it is
drawn.</para>

<para>From there a picture can be written out, replaced, turned, cropped,
adjusted or taken off the page, and the whole document can be brought down to a
target resolution. Turning is lossless: the stored bytes are not touched, only
the matrix that places them, so a fax or a JPEG 2000 picture turns as well as a
JPEG does. A JPEG asked for as a <literal>.jpg</literal> is copied out
compressed, byte for byte, which is the one extraction that loses nothing at
all.</para>

<para>Everything here addresses a picture the way a content stream does, by
page and resource name, which is why picking a row comes first.
<quote>The photograph on page four</quote> is not a question a &PDF; can
answer, and an operation naming one picture applies to every place that page
draws it. Fax and JPEG 2000 pictures can be listed, removed and deduplicated
but not opened, so they cannot be recompressed, adjusted or cropped. Pictures
inside an embedded drawing are not listed at all.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-layout">
<title>Page setup</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Page
Setup…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> (the window calls itself
<guilabel>Page Layout</guilabel>) deals with the five boxes a &PDF; page can
carry, media, crop, bleed, trim and art, which is what a print shop means by
page setup. The table shows them per page in millimetres and says
<guilabel>absent</guilabel> where a page defines none, which is different from
one that matches the media box. The four optional boxes are written as one set,
so a box left unticked is taken away, and you are asked before that happens.
Boxes have to nest, and an arrangement where they do not is refused with the
offender named.</para>

<para>The other tabs resize pages to a named paper, bring every page to one
size, add bleed, draw crop and registration marks, cut pages in two (the
scanned-book fix, where one photograph holds two facing pages), tile one page
across several sheets as a poster, reorder to printer's spreads and back, lay
another document over this one, find the pages that draw nothing and take pages
out, and write the page labels a reader shows instead of 1, 2, 3.</para>

<para>Two things it will not do quietly: resizing scales line widths and
hairlines with the page, and laying one document over another does not merge
their forms, so two forms with a field of the same name keep only the base
document's.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-object-list">
<title>The page as a list</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>The Page as a
List…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> lists what a page draws, each picture, each
block of text, each piece of vector art, topmost first, and lets each one be
moved, resized, turned, recoloured or removed. The boxes on the page and the
rows beside it are the same list twice, so that <quote>the caption near the
bottom</quote> and <quote>object 47</quote> are the same thing. It is kept
alongside the on-page way of doing it
(<xref linkend="page-objects"/>) because a list can be read down, sorted and
searched, and because an object too small to click is still a row here. It can
also add text, a picture or a simple shape to a page.</para>

<para>Changes are collected and written in one pass, for the same reason they
are on the page: an object carries one change at a time.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-typeset">
<title>Setting text as pages</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Set Text as
Pages…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is the one tool here that makes a document
instead of examining one. Type or paste text, or load a file, choose whether to
read it as plain text or as Markdown, and set the paper, the margins, the
columns, the running heads, the typeface, the size, the leading and the
hyphenation. It does not need a document open, and it does not touch the one
that is.</para>

<para>The preview is written by the same engine that writes the finished
document, so where the lines and the pages break on screen is where they will
break in the file, rather than an approximation drawn with a different layout
engine which would be wrong in exactly the places that matter.</para>

<para>Distances across the paper are in millimetres and distances inside the
type are in points, and every box says which, because a margin of 20 and a
leading of 20 are not the same length.</para>

<para>It can only set text in the fourteen fonts every reader knows, which
means western European alphabets: Greek, Cyrillic, Hebrew, Arabic and the CJK
scripts are reported rather than drawn. It does not draw pictures, a Markdown
image contributes its description in italics instead. Tables come out as a
plain ruled grid with no merged cells, no repeated headings and no row split
across a page. There are no footnotes, no cross-references, no index and no
table of contents, and no right-to-left layout.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-modes">
<title>The two menu entries that are not windows</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Move What the Page
Draws…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> and
<menuchoice><guimenu>Forms</guimenu><guimenuitem>Build a
Form…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> open no window. What a page draws is moved by
dragging it, and a form is laid out by drawing it, so both of them take you to
the page with the right mode in force and the properties sidebar open. See
<xref linkend="page-objects"/> and <xref linkend="form-drawing"/>.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Forms</guimenu><guimenuitem>Form Behaviour and
Answers…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is a window and behaves like the ones
above; see <xref linkend="form-behaviour"/>.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="command-line">
<title>The Command Line</title>

<para>Everything the window can do to a document, a script can do too. That is
a design rule of the project rather than a bonus:
<command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> runs the same engine as the window, so a
workflow you worked out by hand can be automated without reaching for another
tool. Nothing is uploaded there either.</para>

<para>This chapter is an orientation. The full reference, every command with
every option, is
<userinput><command>man</command> pdf-smithy-cli</userinput>, and
<userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> <option>--help</option></userinput>
prints the same list at the terminal.</para>

<sect1 id="command-line-shape">
<title>The shape of a command</title>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> <replaceable>command</replaceable> [<replaceable>options</replaceable>] <replaceable>file</replaceable>…</userinput></screen>

<para>The command comes first and is not case-sensitive. Nine of the commands
take a second word saying which of their jobs is meant, so they read as a
sentence:</para>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> colour inspect heft.pdf</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> images list scan.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Those nine are <command>colour</command>, <command>fonts</command>,
<command>images</command>, <command>layout</command>,
<command>objects</command>, <command>field</command>,
<command>preflight</command>, <command>typeset</command> and the conversions.
Each of them ends with a <userinput>limits</userinput> action that says what it
cannot promise, which is worth reading before trusting a result and is
short.</para>

<para><option>-o</option> says where the output goes. Left out, a command that
has to write something writes it beside the input under a new name rather than
over the input, because a command line that overwrites its input by default is
one bad flag away from losing the only copy. A few commands take
<option>-o</option> as a pattern for several files
(<command>split</command>) or as a directory
(<option>--out-dir</option> for <command>batch</command>).</para>

<para><option>--json</option> asks for machine-readable output, and
<option>--limits</option> makes any command say what it cannot promise and do
nothing else.</para>

<warning><para>All the options are registered on one parser, so an option a
command does not use is ignored rather than refused. A flag borrowed from
another command, or misspelt, is accepted in silence and does nothing. Read the
report the command prints rather than assuming the flag took.</para></warning>

<para>Reports go to standard output and errors and progress go to standard
error, so <userinput>2&gt;/dev/null</userinput> is the quiet mode and
<option>--json</option> is the parseable one. The reports are translated like
the rest of the program, so a script that reads them should set
<userinput>LC_ALL=C</userinput> or ask for JSON.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line-commands">
<title>What it can do</title>

<para>Forty-one commands, in seven families.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Documents and pages</term>
<listitem><para><command>info</command>, <command>merge</command>,
<command>split</command>, <command>pages</command> (select, reorder, duplicate
and rotate), <command>compress</command>, <command>linearise</command> (spelt
<command>linearize</command> as well, the way <command>color</command> is
accepted for <command>colour</command>) and <command>set-version</command>.
A version below what the file actually needs is refused and the features that
forced the refusal are named, because lowering the number would remove nothing
and only mislead a reader that then met them.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Things put on pages</term>
<listitem><para><command>sign</command>, <command>watermark</command>,
<command>number</command>, <command>nup</command>, <command>crop</command>,
<command>redact</command>, <command>annotate</command> (list, add, clear,
flatten, and XFDF in and out) and <command>outline</command> (show or rewrite
the table of contents).</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Text</term>
<listitem><para><command>text</command> (show a page's text, or replace a
line), <command>to-text</command>, <command>to-markdown</command>,
<command>to-html</command>, <command>tables</command>,
<command>to-csv</command>, <command>to-svg</command> and
<command>typeset</command>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Pictures</term>
<listitem><para><command>from-images</command>,
<command>export-images</command>, and the <command>images</command> group:
listing, compressing, resampling, replacing, turning, removing, extracting,
adjusting, cropping and merging duplicates.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Forms</term>
<listitem><para><command>form</command> for the everyday jobs, listing, filling
in and flattening, and the <command>field</command> group for building one:
adding, removing, renaming, moving, copying, importing and exporting answers,
gathering many filled-in copies, formatting, validation, calculation, buttons
and tab order.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Looking inside</term>
<listitem><para><command>meta</command>, <command>protect</command>,
<command>fonts</command>, <command>colour</command>, <command>layout</command>,
<command>objects</command> and <command>preflight</command>: the same work the
tool windows do (<xref linkend="tools"/>), one action at a
time.</para></listitem></varlistentry>

<varlistentry><term>Standards, comparing and bulk work</term>
<listitem><para><command>archive</command> (to PDF/A, any of the three levels),
<command>archive-check</command>, <command>to-pdfx</command>,
<command>compare</command>, <command>ocr</command> and
<command>batch</command>. Batch runs its files one after another on purpose:
Ghostscript and Tesseract each take a core, so four at once makes the machine
unusable and finishes no sooner. It never writes over an original, and says so
when it had to skip one.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line-ranges">
<title>Page ranges, coordinates and units</title>

<para>Page ranges accept <userinput>1-4</userinput>, <userinput>8</userinput>,
<userinput>12-last</userinput>, the open-ended <userinput>12-</userinput>,
<userinput>odd</userinput>, <userinput>even</userinput>, and
<userinput>9-1</userinput> to reverse. Order and repetition are preserved, so
<option>--keep</option> <userinput>1,1,2</userinput> writes the first page
twice, which is why duplicating pages needs no command of its own. It is the
same syntax the window uses, on purpose.</para>

<para>Coordinates are in points (1/72 inch) measured from the bottom-left
corner of the page <emphasis>as it is shown</emphasis>, with any rotation
already applied, which is what a ruler held against a viewer measures. An area
is written <userinput>page:left,bottom,width,height</userinput>.</para>

<para>Lengths carry one unit per option name, and the help text says which. A
name never means points to one command and millimetres to another: everything
spelled <option>--margin</option>, <option>--top</option>,
<option>--bottom</option>, <option>--left</option>, <option>--right</option>,
<option>--gutter</option>, <option>--sheet-margin</option> or
<option>--font-size</option> is points, and where the trade talks in
millimetres the option says so, so <option>--bleed</option>,
<option>--mark-length</option> and <option>--overlap</option> are millimetres,
and the margins <command>typeset</command> puts round a page are
<option>--margin-mm</option> and its four siblings. Where a number may carry
its own unit, <userinput>18pt</userinput>, <userinput>5mm</userinput>,
<userinput>0.5cm</userinput>, <userinput>0.25in</userinput>, it is taken at its
word. A paper size given as a pair of numbers must carry its unit, so
<option>--size</option> <userinput>210x297mm</userinput> is A4 and
<option>--size</option> <userinput>595x842pt</userinput> is the same page
measured the other way; <userinput>210x297</userinput> on its own is turned
down rather than guessed at. Named papers such as <userinput>a4</userinput> and
<userinput>letter</userinput> need no unit.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line-exit">
<title>Exit codes</title>

<para>Written so that a command can be used in a shell condition the way
<command>diff</command> is.</para>

<table id="command-line-exit-table">
<title>What the exit status means</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Code</entry><entry>Meaning</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>0</entry><entry>It worked, and nothing was found.</entry></row>
<row><entry>1</entry><entry>Differences or findings. <command>compare</command>,
<command>archive-check</command>, <command>preflight</command> and
<command>fonts check</command> all report this way.</entry></row>
<row><entry>2</entry><entry>The command line was not understood, including an
unknown command and no arguments at all.</entry></row>
<row><entry>3</entry><entry>The input could not be read, or part of the work
was refused and reported.</entry></row>
<row><entry>4</entry><entry>The output could not be written.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<para>So a print job can be gated on a check:</para>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> preflight job.pdf &amp;&amp; lp job.pdf</userinput></screen>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line-examples">
<title>Worked examples</title>

<para>One file per invoice:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> split invoices.pdf <option>--every</option> 1</userinput></screen>

<para>Keep some pages of a report, turn one of them, and write the rest away:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> pages report.pdf <option>--keep</option> 1-3,7,12-last <option>--rotate</option> 4:90 <option>-o</option> short.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Make a crooked German scan searchable and straight, and then small enough
to email:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> ocr scan.pdf <option>--languages</option> deu+eng <option>--straighten</option> <option>-o</option> searchable.pdf</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> compress searchable.pdf <option>--level</option> strong <option>-o</option> small.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Make a whole folder of scans searchable, keeping the originals:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> batch scans/*.pdf <option>--op</option> ocr <option>--out-dir</option> searchable/</userinput></screen>

<para>Take a name out of a document for good, removed rather than covered over.
The area is page, then left, bottom, width and height in points:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> redact case.pdf <option>--area</option> 1:72,700,180,16 <option>-o</option> public.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Bates-stamp a disclosure bundle:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> number bundle.pdf <option>--text</option> {bates} <option>--bates</option> ACME- <option>-o</option> stamped.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Fill a form in and then make the answers permanent:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> form application.pdf <option>--set</option> Name=Bueng <option>--set</option> Town=Bern <option>-o</option> filled.pdf</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> form filled.pdf <option>--flatten</option> <option>-o</option> final.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>See what changed between two drafts. The exit status is nonzero when
something did, so a script can act on it:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> compare contract-v1.pdf contract-v2.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Convert for an archive only if it is not already archival:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> archive-check invoice.pdf &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 \
      || <command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> archive invoice.pdf <option>--level</option> 2b <option>-o</option> invoice-archival.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Prepare a cover for a printing press. Ghostscript does the conversion, so
the command says plainly when it is not installed rather than writing something
that only looks like PDF/X. The profile is the printing condition the job will
be run under; left out, a CMYK profile on this system is looked for and its
absence reported:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> to-pdfx cover.pdf <option>--pdfx-level</option> x1a <option>--icc-profile</option> /usr/share/color/icc/ISOcoated_v2.icc <option>-o</option> cover-print.pdf</userinput></screen>

<para>Read a table off a page before trusting it, then write it out. There is
no table in a &PDF;, only text that lines up, so the confidence figure is worth
reading first:</para>
<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> tables report.pdf <option>--pages</option> 3-5</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput><command>pdf-smithy-cli</command> to-csv report.pdf <option>--pages</option> 3-5 <option>-o</option> figures.csv</userinput></screen>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<chapter id="shortcuts">
<title>Keyboard Shortcuts</title>

<para>All of these can be changed under
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Keyboard
Shortcuts…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>

<table id="shortcuts-modes">
<title>Choosing what you are here to do</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>Command</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycap>F5</keycap></entry><entry>Read &amp; Fill In</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F6</keycap></entry><entry>Edit the Page</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F7</keycap></entry><entry>Edit the Form</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F8</keycap></entry><entry>Arrange the Pages</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Enter;</keycombo></entry><entry>Keep the Changes on the Page</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<table id="shortcuts-files">
<title>Files</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>Command</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>N</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>New</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Open</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>I</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Insert File…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Save</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Save As</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>E</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Extract Pages to New File…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>P</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Print</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>W</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Close</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Q</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Quit</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<table id="shortcuts-edit">
<title>Editing</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>Command</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Z</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Undo</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>Z</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Redo</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>X</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Cut</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>C</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Copy</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>V</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Paste</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>A</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Select All, meaning every page of the document</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<table id="shortcuts-pages">
<title>Pages</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>Command</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>L</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Rotate Left</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Rotate Right</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>D</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Duplicate Pages</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Del;</entry><entry>Delete Pages, where the page grid is showing</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Recognise Text (OCR)…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>G</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Sign…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>E</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Correct Text…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Comments…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Move What the Page Draws…</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>P</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Check the Document…</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<table id="shortcuts-view">
<title>Looking at the document</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>Command</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Home;</keycombo></entry><entry>First Page</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&End;</keycombo></entry><entry>Last Page</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>1</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Fit Width</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>2</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Fit Height</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>One Whole Page</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>4</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Two Pages Side by Side</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>0</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Free Zoom</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>+</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Larger Pages</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>-</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Smaller Pages</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F4</keycap></entry><entry>Properties Sidebar</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>B</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Contents Sidebar</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F9</keycap></entry><entry>Page Thumbnails</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Show the menu bar, or hide it</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<para>These next ones are not commands and are not in the shortcut editor. They
belong to whatever has the keyboard at the time, which is what lets one key
mean the right thing in four different places.</para>

<table id="shortcuts-keys">
<title>Keys that belong to the page</title>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Key</entry><entry>What it does</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>&PgUp;, &PgDn;</entry><entry>Glide up or down a little less than a windowful</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>Up</keycap>, <keycap>Down</keycap></entry><entry>Glide a line</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Ctrl; and the wheel</entry><entry>Make the pages larger or smaller, around the pointer</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Space; held</entry><entry>Drag the page about without changing tool</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Ctrl; and a drag</entry><entry>Take a box of the page rather than its words</entry></row>
<row><entry>Double click</entry><entry>Select the word under the pointer</entry></row>
<row><entry>Triple click</entry><entry>Select the line</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Esc;</entry><entry>Let go: clears the selection, cancels a mark being drawn, leaves a line without keeping what was typed, disarms a field kind</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Tab;, <keycombo action="simul">&Shift;&Tab;</keycombo></entry><entry>The next or previous form field to fill in, or the next or previous line to correct</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Enter;</entry><entry>Finish a one-line answer or a correction and move on</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Space;</entry><entry>Tick the form field that is picked</entry></row>
<row><entry>Arrow keys</entry><entry>Move what is chosen a point at a time, or step through a group of radio buttons</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Shift;</keycombo> and the arrow keys</entry><entry>Move what is chosen ten points at a time</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Del;, &Backspace;</entry><entry>Remove what is chosen on the page: an object, a comment, a form field</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Shift; and a corner handle</entry><entry>Free the proportions of an object, or keep the proportions of a form field</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Shift; and the turn handle</entry><entry>Snap the turn to fifteen degrees</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

</chapter>


